self_contained_problem:
Let $(R,\mathfrak m)$ be a regular local ring of dimension $n$, and let $I\subseteq R$ be an ideal whose big height (the maximum of the heights of the associated primes of $R/I$) equals $e$. For a positive integer $m$, denote by $I^{(m)}$ the $m$-th symbolic power of $I$, defined as $I^{(m)}=\bigcap_{\mathfrak p\in\mathrm{Ass}(R/I)}(I^m R_{\mathfrak p}\cap R)$. Determine whether the following four strengthened symbolic-power containments hold for every integer $r\ge 1$:

(1) $I^{(m)}\subseteq \mathfrak m^{rn-r}\,I^{r}$ whenever $m\ge rn$;

(2) $I^{(m)}\subseteq \mathfrak m^{rn-r-(n-1)}\,I^{r}$ whenever $m\ge rn-(n-1)$;

(3) $I^{(m)}\subseteq \mathfrak m^{re-r}\,I^{r}$ whenever $m\ge re$;

(4) $I^{(m)}\subseteq \mathfrak m^{re-r-(e-1)}\,I^{r}$ whenever $m\ge re-(e-1)$.

Consider LiHoF4, an insulating dipolar Ising ferromagnet in which the Ho3+ magnetic moments order below a Curie temperature T_c ≈ 1.53 K with the moments aligned along the crystalline c axis. Treat the system as a three-dimensional dipolar Ising ferromagnet on the LiHoF4 lattice. Theoretical analyses of such dipolar Ising ferromagnets predict that:

(a) the ground-state magnetic domain configuration consists of parallel, antiferromagnetically aligned sheets (alternating slabs of opposite magnetization along c);
(b) the characteristic width of these sheets grows as the demagnetization factor of the sample decreases, so that in the limit of an infinitely long, thin needle-shaped sample a state of uniform magnetization is realized;
(c) for sufficiently thick samples, the sheet domains develop a branched (tree-like) pattern near the surfaces, consistent with the Privman–Lifshitz / Landau-type branching scenario for uniaxial dipolar ferromagnets.

Problem. Determine, by direct experimental observation, the actual low-temperature (T < T_c) magnetic domain structure of pure (stoichiometric) LiHoF4 as a function of sample geometry and demagnetization factor, and decide whether the predicted phenomena (a)–(c) — parallel antiferromagnetically aligned sheet domains, monotonic dependence of sheet width on demagnetization factor with uniform magnetization in the needle limit, and branching of the domain pattern in thick samples — are quantitatively realized in this material.

Consider the nonequilibrium Green's function (NEGF) formalism for an interacting many-fermion system, in which the one-particle Green's function G(t,t') on the Keldysh contour satisfies the Kadanoff–Baym equations (KBE)

   (i ∂_t − h(t)) G(t,t') = δ_C(t,t') + ∫_C Σ[G](t,t̄) G(t̄,t') dt̄,

with a self-energy Σ[G] generated by a Φ-derivable (conserving) many-body approximation such as the second Born approximation, the GW approximation, or the T-matrix approximation. Such approximations preserve macroscopic conservation laws (particle number, energy, momentum), yet exhibit the following well-documented pathologies when used to propagate dynamics:

  (i) breakdown of the perturbative expansion in regimes of strong two-body interaction;
  (ii) an artificial correlation-induced damping of the time evolution that, in finite systems, drives the dynamics into a non-decaying steady state, and in extended systems produces arbitrarily long-lived states relaxing to a unique steady state of unclear physical status;
  (iii) violation of positivity (and related spectral) constraints on two-particle correlation functions, e.g. failure of density–density response functions or reduced density matrices to remain positive semidefinite.

Problem. Identify, in a mathematically precise way:
  (a) the parameter regimes (interaction strength, density, system size, time scales) in which each of the conserving approximations above can be guaranteed to yield qualitatively correct KBE dynamics;
  (b) corrective principles or modifications of the self-energy functional Σ[G] (or of the propagation scheme) that simultaneously retain the conserving (Φ-derivable) property and restore the missing physical requirements—namely absence of spurious damping, physically meaningful steady states, and positivity of two-particle correlation functions;
  (c) a characterization of which of the long-lived/steady states produced by KBE+MBA in extended systems are genuine features of the underlying many-body dynamics versus artifacts of the approximation.

In group-based cryptography, a "platform group" is an infinite, finitely presented (typically non-abelian) group $G$ used as the underlying algebraic structure for cryptographic primitives such as key-exchange protocols (e.g. the Anshel–Anshel–Goldfeld protocol or the Ko–Lee/Diffie–Hellman-type conjugacy protocol), authentication schemes, and digital signatures. For such a scheme to be both implementable and secure, the platform group $G$ should satisfy a list of competing requirements, including:

(i) elements of $G$ admit a unique, efficiently computable normal form, so that the group can be represented faithfully on a computer;
(ii) the word problem in $G$ is solvable in (preferably low-degree) polynomial time;
(iii) multiplication, inversion, and (where needed) sampling of "random" elements of prescribed length can be performed efficiently;
(iv) certain algorithmic problems whose presumed hardness underlies the proposed cryptographic primitive — for example, the conjugacy search problem, the simultaneous conjugacy search problem, the subgroup membership problem, the geodesic length problem, the decomposition problem, or the twisted conjugacy search problem — are computationally intractable on average for instances generated by a specified distribution, and resist known length-based, linear-algebraic, quotient, and generic-case attacks;
(v) $G$ admits a sufficiently rich supply of pairs of commuting (or otherwise compatible) subgroups $A,B \le G$, or other algebraic features, required by the specific protocol.

Problem. Determine and rigorously justify a class $\mathcal{C}$ of finitely presented non-abelian groups that is "most appropriate" as a source of platform groups for non-commutative group-based cryptography, in the sense that:

(a) every $G \in \mathcal{C}$ satisfies (i)–(iii) above with explicit complexity bounds;
(b) there exists a cryptographic primitive whose security can be reduced to an algorithmic problem in $G$ that is provably, or at least convincingly conjecturally, hard on average for $G \in \mathcal{C}$, and which is not broken by currently known generic attacks (length-based attacks, linear representation/linearization attacks, quotient attacks, subgroup attacks);
(c) the class $\mathcal{C}$ is shown to be preferable, by stated criteria, to previously proposed candidate classes such as braid groups $B_n$, Thompson's group $F$, polycyclic groups, free metabelian groups, small cancellation groups, hyperbolic groups, right-angled Artin groups, Grigorchuk-type self-similar groups, and matrix groups over suitable rings.

Either exhibit such a class $\mathcal{C}$ together with a complete analysis establishing (a)–(c), or prove that no class of finitely presented groups can simultaneously satisfy (a)–(c).

Observed gamma-ray burst (GRB) prompt emission spectra are commonly fit by the empirical Band function, a smoothly broken power law with photon-number density

N(E) = A · (E/100 keV)^α · exp(−E/E₀),  for E ≤ (α − β)E₀,
N(E) = A · ((α − β)E₀/100 keV)^(α − β) · exp(β − α) · (E/100 keV)^β,  for E > (α − β)E₀,

characterized by a low-energy photon index α, a high-energy photon index β, and a peak energy E_peak = (2 + α)E₀ in the νF_ν representation. Typical observed values are α ≈ −1, β ≈ −2.3, and E_peak in the range from tens of keV to about 1 MeV.

Identify the physical radiation mechanism (or combination of mechanisms) responsible for producing such spectra during the prompt phase of GRBs. Candidate mechanisms to be considered include:
(i) optically thin synchrotron emission from shock-accelerated relativistic leptons in an ordered or turbulent magnetic field;
(ii) jitter radiation from leptons traversing small-scale magnetic turbulence;
(iii) synchrotron self-Compton (SSC) emission;
(iv) Comptonization of thermal or photospheric (quasi-blackbody) seed photons by sub-relativistic or mildly relativistic electrons;
(v) hadronic radiation processes (e.g., proton synchrotron or photo-pion–induced cascades).

The proposed mechanism must simultaneously account for (a) the distribution of observed low-energy spectral slopes α, including values harder than the synchrotron fast-cooling limit α = −3/2 and the slow-cooling limit α = −2/3; (b) the cooling regime of the radiating particles consistent with observed spectral evolution and energy budgets; and (c) the observed clustering and distribution of peak energies E_peak.

Determine which mechanism is correct, or establish a definitive multi-component model that reproduces the empirical Band-function phenomenology under these constraints.

In the framework of semi-analytic modeling of galaxy formation — where galaxy properties (stellar mass, star-formation rate, cold gas content, etc.) are computed by applying parameterized physical recipes for gas cooling, star formation, stellar/AGN feedback, and galaxy merging on top of dark-matter halo merger trees extracted from a high-resolution cosmological N-body simulation — determine whether there exists a single such model, with physically plausible recipes and without resorting to unrestricted free tuning of every recipe (including the cooling prescription), that simultaneously reproduces all of the following observational constraints:

(i) the observed relation between specific star-formation rate (sSFR ≡ SFR/M_*) and stellar mass M_* of galaxies at redshift z = 0;
(ii) the galaxy stellar mass function (SMF) at z = 0 and its evolution with redshift;
(iii) galaxy two-point correlation functions (clustering as a function of stellar mass and/or luminosity);
(iv) cold-gas (atomic + molecular) mass fractions of galaxies as a function of stellar mass.

Either exhibit such a model and demonstrate the simultaneous fit, or show that within the standard SAM framework no such consistent parameter choice exists.

In semi-analytic models (SAMs) of galaxy formation, dark matter halo merger trees are populated with baryonic galaxies whose evolution is governed by simplified prescriptions for gas cooling, star formation, supernova and AGN feedback, mergers, and chemical enrichment. Current SAMs that successfully reproduce observed stellar mass functions, cosmic star formation rate histories, and galaxy two-point clustering statistics tend to predict cold (atomic + molecular) gas mass functions in galaxies that lie substantially above observational determinations, especially at low halo masses.

Identify a physically plausible additional baryonic process, or a corrected gas-cooling prescription, that can be incorporated into a SAM so that, simultaneously:
(i) the predicted cold gas mass function in galaxies agrees with observations across the relevant mass range;
(ii) the predicted galaxy stellar mass function is preserved at the level of agreement currently achieved;
(iii) the predicted star-formation rate distribution as a function of stellar mass and redshift is preserved;
(iv) the predicted galaxy clustering statistics remain consistent with observations.

Candidate directions to be evaluated include: (a) a preheating mechanism that raises the entropy of intergalactic gas so that it cannot cool into low-mass haloes; (b) a feedback channel that preferentially heats low-entropy gas in high-redshift haloes; (c) a revised treatment of radiative cooling that suppresses currently over-efficient cooling. Determine whether such a process exists, and if so specify the prescription (functional form, energy or entropy budget, halo-mass and redshift dependence) and demonstrate that conditions (i)–(iv) are jointly satisfied.

A holomorphic local dynamical system at the origin of $\mathbb{C}^n$ is a germ $f\colon (\mathbb{C}^n,0)\to(\mathbb{C}^n,0)$ of a holomorphic self-map fixing the origin. Two such germs $f$ and $g$ are said to be:
- holomorphically locally conjugate if there exists a germ of biholomorphism $\varphi\colon(\mathbb{C}^n,0)\to(\mathbb{C}^n,0)$ with $\varphi\circ f=g\circ\varphi$;
- $C^k$ locally conjugate ($k\in\mathbb{N}\cup\{\infty\}$) if such a $\varphi$ exists as a germ of $C^k$ diffeomorphism;
- topologically locally conjugate if such a $\varphi$ exists as a germ of homeomorphism.

A class of invariants is a rule $\mathcal{I}$ associating to every holomorphic local dynamical system $f$ at the origin of $\mathbb{C}^n$ an object $\mathcal{I}(f)$ (drawn from some specified set of mathematical objects). The class $\mathcal{I}$ is called complete for a given equivalence relation (holomorphic, $C^k$, smooth, or topological local conjugacy) if for every pair $f$, $g$ of holomorphic local dynamical systems at $0\in\mathbb{C}^n$ one has
$$ \mathcal{I}(f)=\mathcal{I}(g)\iff f \text{ and } g \text{ are locally conjugate in the chosen sense.}$$

Problem. For each $n\ge 1$, construct an explicit, possibly computable class of invariants $\mathcal{I}$ of holomorphic local dynamical systems at $0\in\mathbb{C}^n$ that is complete with respect to:
(a) holomorphic local conjugacy;
(b) smooth ($C^\infty$) local conjugacy;
(c) $C^k$ local conjugacy for finite $k\ge 1$;
(d) topological local conjugacy.

The class of invariants should be as small and as explicitly computable as possible.

In the standard three-flavour neutrino oscillation framework, the three known neutrino flavour eigenstates (electron, muon, tau) are unitary linear combinations of three mass eigenstates with masses m_1, m_2, m_3, related by the PMNS mixing matrix and characterized by two independent mass-squared splittings Δm²_21 ≈ 7.5×10⁻⁵ eV² and |Δm²_31| ≈ 2.5×10⁻³ eV², three mixing angles, and one CP-violating phase. Lorentz invariance and CPT invariance are assumed, so neutrinos and antineutrinos share the same mass spectrum and oscillation parameters, neutrinos travel at speeds indistinguishable from c at experimental precision, and matter effects are described by the Standard Model charged- and neutral-current interactions.

A number of experimental results are in tension with this minimal picture:
(a) the LSND excess of ν̄_e appearance in a ν̄_μ beam at L/E ~ 1 m/MeV;
(b) the MiniBooNE excess of ν̄_e- and ν_e-like events in ν̄_μ and ν_μ beams, including a low-energy excess;
(c) the reactor antineutrino anomaly: a deficit of ν̄_e at short baselines relative to recomputed reactor flux predictions;
(d) the gallium anomaly: a deficit of ν_e events in radiochemical source calibrations of GALLEX/SAGE-type detectors;
(e) reported asymmetries between neutrino and antineutrino oscillation parameters;
(f) possible seasonal/temporal variations in observed rates;
(g) a reported anomalous superluminal propagation velocity for muon neutrinos over a long baseline.

Question. Determine whether the anomalies (a)–(g) reflect genuine physics beyond the three-flavour oscillation framework. If so, construct a single quantitative theoretical extension—such as the addition of one or more sterile neutrino states mixing with the active flavours, CPT-violating mass spectra distinguishing ν from ν̄, or non-standard neutrino–matter interactions parametrized by Hermitian couplings ε_{αβ}—that simultaneously fits the magnitudes, energy and baseline dependences of all anomalies (a)–(g) while remaining consistent with: all null short- and long-baseline oscillation searches, atmospheric and solar neutrino data, cosmological bounds on extra relativistic species and on the sum of neutrino masses, and existing tests of Lorentz invariance and of the speed of neutrinos. Either exhibit such a model with explicit parameter values and predicted observables, or prove that no such single model exists within the stated constraints.

Let $X$ be a compact Hausdorff space and let $S(X)$ denote the semigroup of all continuous self-maps $X \to X$ under composition. A subgroup $H \subseteq S(X)$ (i.e., a subgroup of the group of units of $S(X)$, consisting of self-homeomorphisms) is called:

- transitive on $X$ if for every $x, y \in X$ there exists $h \in H$ with $h(x) = y$;
- distal if for any two distinct points $x_1, x_2 \in X$ and any point $x \in X$, there exists an open neighborhood $U$ of $x$ such that $\{h(x_1), h(x_2)\} \not\subset U$ for every $h \in H$;
- left bounded if there exists a finite set $F \subseteq S(X)$ such that $H \subseteq \bigcup_{f \in F} f \cdot H'$ in an appropriate sense making $H$ "bounded" with respect to left translation (equivalently, satisfying the standard left-boundedness condition under which transitive left-bounded subgroups of $S(X)$ are known to be distal).

It is known that every left bounded subgroup $H \subseteq S(X)$ that acts transitively on $X$ is distal. 

Question: Conversely, must every distal subgroup $H \subseteq S(X)$ that acts transitively on the compact space $X$ be left bounded?

Consider a finite nonrelativistic many-electron quantum system governed by a time-dependent Hamiltonian H(t) = T + V_ext(t) + W, where T is the kinetic energy operator, W is the two-body Coulomb interaction, and V_ext(t) is a (possibly time-dependent) one-body external potential. Let γ(x,x';t) denote the one-body reduced density matrix of the time-evolving N-electron pure state, with spectral decomposition γ(x,x';t) = Σ_k n_k(t) φ_k(x,t) φ_k*(x',t), where n_k(t) ∈ [0,1] are the natural occupation numbers and {φ_k(·,t)} are the natural orbitals.

In time-dependent reduced density matrix functional theory (TDRDMFT) and time-dependent density functional theory (TDDFT), one approximates the exchange-correlation (or interaction) energy/potential as a functional of γ(t) (or of the density n(x,t)) so as to close the equations of motion for γ(t) (or for n(x,t)). Most existing approximations are "adiabatic": they take a ground-state functional E_xc[γ] and evaluate it at the instantaneous γ(t), thereby producing a functional that is local in time, ignores memory effects, and depends only on |c_k|² type quantities (so that signs/phases of natural-orbital coefficients are fixed). Such adiabatic functionals are known to lead to pathologies, including occupation numbers n_k(t) that remain constant in time even when the exact dynamics dictate that they change, and they generally fail to conserve the total energy ⟨H(t)⟩ when V_ext is time-independent.

Problem. Construct an exchange-correlation functional Φ_xc[γ(t'), t' ≤ t] for the equation of motion of γ(t) (or, in the TDDFT setting, an xc potential v_xc[n(t'), t' ≤ t](x,t)) such that:

(i) it is genuinely nonlocal in space and nonlocal (memory-dependent) in time;
(ii) it explicitly depends on the time-dependent phases of the natural-orbital expansion coefficients, not merely on their moduli, so that the resulting equations of motion permit n_k(t) to vary in time as required by the exact dynamics;
(iii) for any time-independent external potential V_ext, the total energy ⟨H⟩ is conserved exactly along the approximate evolution;
(iv) the functional reduces to a known accurate ground-state xc functional in the static limit.

Either exhibit such a functional and prove that it satisfies (i)–(iv), or prove that no functional of γ(t) alone (without auxiliary memory variables) can satisfy (i)–(iv) simultaneously.

A finite projective plane of order q (with q ≥ 2 an integer) is a finite incidence structure consisting of a set of "points" and a set of "lines" together with an incidence relation between them, satisfying:
(i) any two distinct points lie on exactly one common line;
(ii) any two distinct lines meet in exactly one common point;
(iii) there exist four points, no three of which are collinear;
(iv) every line contains exactly q + 1 points (equivalently, every point lies on exactly q + 1 lines).

It is known that a finite projective plane of order q exists whenever q is a prime power. The Bruck–Ryser–Chowla theorem rules out certain values, and computer-assisted proofs have ruled out q = 10. For other small non–prime-power orders, existence remains undetermined.

Problem: Classify all finite projective planes, or at least determine the complete set of integers q ≥ 2 that occur as orders of finite projective planes. In particular, decide whether there exists a finite projective plane of order q = 12.

In relativistic heavy-ion collisions, the transverse initial-state energy (or entropy) density profile e(r,φ) in the plane perpendicular to the beam axis is commonly characterized by the eccentricity coefficients

  ε_n e^{i n Φ_n} = − ( ∫ r dr dφ \, r^n e^{i n φ} \, e(r,φ) ) / ( ∫ r dr dφ \, r^n e(r,φ) ),  n = 1, 2, 3, …,

i.e. the first few coordinate-space Fourier moments of the radial profile. These coefficients capture the gross transverse anisotropy of the initial geometry but ignore (i) the dependence of the energy density on the longitudinal (beam-direction) coordinate η, (ii) the initial transverse and longitudinal flow velocity field u^μ(x), and (iii) finer transverse structure such as radial profile, fluctuations on multiple scales, and correlations between layers at different rapidity.

Propose a set of scalar quantities (functionals of the initial energy/entropy density e(τ_0, x_⊥, η) and of the initial velocity field u^μ(τ_0, x_⊥, η) on a fixed initial proper-time hypersurface τ = τ_0) that:

1. extends the ε_n expansion so as to characterize the longitudinal η-dependence of e and u^μ, including longitudinal decorrelation between different rapidity slices;
2. characterizes the initial transverse and longitudinal velocity profile of u^μ;
3. captures additional transverse features (radial profile, multi-scale fluctuations, cumulants beyond the first Fourier moment) not encoded by ε_n alone;

and that, taken together, form a basis (in a sense to be made precise) sufficient to predict, to a specified accuracy, the experimentally measurable hadronic flow observables v_n{k}, event-plane correlations, and rapidity-dependent two-particle correlations produced by subsequent hydrodynamic evolution.

In relativistic heavy-ion collisions, the azimuthal distribution of produced particles is characterized by Fourier flow coefficients v_n. For each event one defines v_n via dN/dφ ∝ 1 + 2 Σ_{n≥1} v_n cos(n(φ − Ψ_n)), and the two-particle cumulant flow harmonic v_n{2} is defined through the two-particle azimuthal correlator ⟨⟨cos(n(φ_1 − φ_2))⟩⟩ = v_n{2}^2, with higher-order cumulants v_n{4}, v_n{6}, … extracted from multi-particle correlators in the standard cumulant expansion. Soft physics refers to the low transverse-momentum p_T regime (p_T ≲ 2 GeV) typically modeled by relativistic hydrodynamics applied to a fluctuating initial state, while hard physics refers to the high-p_T regime governed by hard parton production and energy loss in the produced medium.

Construct a single, unified theoretical framework (with a fixed set of physical parameters, including initial-state model, transport coefficients, equation of state, and energy-loss prescription) that, in one calculation, simultaneously reproduces, within experimental uncertainties, the following observables in heavy-ion collisions at a given collision energy:
(i) the flow harmonics v_n{2}(p_T, centrality) for n = 2, 3, 4, … as functions of transverse momentum and centrality;
(ii) the higher-order cumulants v_n{4}, v_n{6}, v_n{8} and the corresponding cumulant ratios for the same n and centralities;
(iii) observables spanning both the soft (low-p_T) and hard (high-p_T) regimes over a large range in transverse momentum, including the nuclear modification factor R_AA(p_T) and high-p_T azimuthal anisotropies.

Either exhibit such a framework or prove that no such single calculation with one fixed parameter set can simultaneously match all of (i)–(iii).

Consider relativistic heavy-ion collisions in which the azimuthal distribution of final-state particles in a single event is expanded in a Fourier series

  dN/dφ ∝ 1 + 2 Σ_{n≥1} v_n cos[ n(φ − Ψ_n) ],

where v_n ≥ 0 are the anisotropic flow harmonics and Ψ_n are the corresponding event-plane angles. Because of finite-multiplicity event-by-event fluctuations and non-flow correlations, experimentally accessible flow observables depend on the analysis method used. Standard methods include:

 (i) the scalar-product method, based on Q-vectors Q_n = Σ_j e^{i n φ_j} and yielding a quantity v_n{SP};
 (ii) two-particle correlation methods, returning v_n{2} = √⟨⟨ e^{i n(φ_1 − φ_2)} ⟩⟩;
 (iii) multi-particle cumulant methods of order 2k, returning v_n{2k} obtained from the cumulants c_n{2k} of the 2k-particle azimuthal correlations;
 (iv) event-plane methods with finite resolution R_n, returning v_n{EP}.

Each method gives, in general, a different moment or function of the underlying event-by-event probability distribution P(v_n, Ψ_n) of the flow vector V_n = v_n e^{i n Ψ_n}.

Problem. Given a theoretical model (for example a hydrodynamic or hybrid simulation) that produces, for an ensemble of events, the single-event particle distributions dN_e/dφ together with their multiplicities, formulate explicit estimators that reproduce, on the theoretical side, the observables v_n{SP}, v_n{2}, v_n{2k} (for k = 2, 3, 4), and v_n{EP} as measured experimentally — including the effects of finite multiplicity, finite event-plane resolution, and any kinematic cuts and weights used in the experimental analyses — so that the resulting theoretical numbers can be compared apples-to-apples with the corresponding experimental values. In particular, identify under what conditions on P(v_n, Ψ_n) and on the non-flow contributions these different estimators agree or differ, and quantify the leading corrections.

Consider relativistic heavy-ion collisions modeled by an initial-state geometry with event-by-event fluctuations followed by hydrodynamic evolution. The transverse momentum spectrum of produced hadrons is characterized by its azimuthal Fourier decomposition $dN/d\phi \propto 1 + 2\sum_{n\ge 1} v_n \cos(n(\phi-\Psi_n))$, where $v_n$ are the harmonic flow coefficients and $\Psi_n$ the event-plane angles. Mean transverse-momentum fluctuations are quantified by event-by-event variations of $\langle p_T\rangle$, which are sensitive to the number $N_s$ of independent sources (e.g., participant nucleons or hot spots) entering the initial-state energy/entropy density. 

Develop theoretical calculations within a chosen initial-condition plus viscous hydrodynamic framework that:

(i) predict $\langle p_T\rangle$ event-by-event fluctuations with statistical precision sufficient to discriminate between models with different numbers of initial-state sources $N_s$;

(ii) compute higher harmonic flow coefficients $v_n$ ($n \ge 2$) at center-of-mass energies below top RHIC energies (i.e., across the RHIC beam energy scan) and for collision systems other than Au+Au and Pb+Pb, in particular Cu+Cu and U+U;

(iii) provide identified-particle (e.g., $\pi^\pm$, $K^\pm$, $p,\bar p$) flow harmonics $v_n(p_T)$ as functions of transverse momentum.

Determine whether such calculations can be carried out with sufficient statistical accuracy to meaningfully constrain $N_s$ and to extend the model–data comparison beyond top-energy collisions.

Consider a finite-dimensional bipartite quantum system consisting of a target system S coupled to an auxiliary (ancilla/environment) system A, with joint Hilbert space H_S ⊗ H_A. Equip the relevant state manifold (e.g., the unitary group U(H_S ⊗ H_A), the projective Hilbert space, or the manifold of density operators with the Bures or Fubini–Study metric, as appropriate to the chosen control task) with a Riemannian metric induced by the available control resources, so that admissible evolutions are smooth curves whose lengths measure control cost (time, energy, or noise sensitivity). 

For each of the following concrete quantum-control objectives, characterize explicitly the geodesics of the corresponding metric that realize the objective:
(i) implementing a prescribed unitary gate on S;
(ii) preparing a prescribed pure or mixed state of S;
(iii) suppressing a specified decoherence channel acting on S via reversible or irreversible coupling to A;
(iv) maximizing transfer of a chosen quantity (e.g., entanglement, information) between S and A.

Then determine, quantitatively, the extent to which these optimal geodesic evolutions can be approximated by Hamiltonians and dissipators that are physically available in mesoscopic quantum systems (e.g., bilinear spin–boson couplings, bounded-strength two-body interactions, locally addressable controls). In particular, provide bounds on the achievable approximation error (in an appropriate operator or trace-distance norm) as a function of the available interaction set, control bandwidth, and coupling strengths.

In an active galactic nucleus (AGN), gas accretes onto a super-massive black hole (SMBH) through an accretion disk, and in some sources a highly collimated, relativistic jet is launched approximately perpendicular to the disk plane. Develop a physical and mathematical theory that determines the coupling between the accretion disk and the relativistic jet. Specifically, identify the mechanism (e.g., magnetohydrodynamic extraction of rotational energy from the black hole and/or the disk, large-scale poloidal magnetic field configurations, radiation pressure, or other) that:

(i) explains under what conditions on the SMBH mass M, spin a, accretion rate \dot{M}, and the magnetic flux threading the disk and horizon a relativistic jet of bulk Lorentz factor \Gamma \gg 1 is launched and collimated;
(ii) predicts the jet power P_jet as a quantitative function of these disk and black-hole parameters;
(iii) accounts for why only a fraction of AGN with similar accretion properties produce powerful relativistic jets while the remainder do not.

Provide a self-consistent model (or rigorous criterion) characterizing this disk–jet coupling, and derive the resulting scaling laws relating jet power, jet Lorentz factor, and collimation angle to the underlying accretion-disk and black-hole parameters.

Consider an active galactic nucleus (AGN), modeled as a supermassive black hole at the center of a galaxy surrounded by an accretion flow that emits an ionizing continuum. Around this central engine there exists a broad-line region (BLR), consisting of photoionized gas clouds whose Doppler-broadened permitted emission lines (e.g., the hydrogen Balmer series) have full widths of order 10^3–10^4 km/s. Farther out, beyond a dust sublimation radius, lies an obscuring structure commonly described as a (possibly clumpy) molecular torus, which produces orientation-dependent obscuration in the unified model of AGN. Velocity-resolved reverberation mapping in some sources suggests a disk-like BLR geometry with kinematic signatures of inflow.

Provide a physically and geometrically consistent description of:

(a) the spatial geometry, kinematics, and cloud distribution of the broad-line region (e.g., disk-like vs. spherical, presence of inflow, outflow, or rotation);

(b) the structure of the obscuring torus, including whether it is a smooth or clumpy medium, its inner (sublimation) radius, vertical extent, and covering factor;

(c) the physical mechanism connecting the BLR to the toroidal obscuration, in particular how the AGN ionizing continuum interacting with a clumpy dusty medium and with the molecular torus beyond the sublimation radius regulates the transition between the BLR and the torus.

The problem is to determine such a model that is consistent with observed reverberation-mapping responses, infrared spectral energy distributions of the torus, and the type-1/type-2 AGN dichotomy.

Background. In the standard treatment of compound-nuclear (CN) reactions, an incident projectile and target form a long-lived intermediate (compound) nucleus that subsequently decays into one of several open channels. Two complementary descriptions are commonly used:

(i) An individual resonance description, in which each isolated CN resonance is parameterized (e.g., via R-matrix theory) by its energy, total width, and partial widths into each channel. This requires the number of resonances in the energy window of interest to be small enough to be enumerated and fit.

(ii) A statistical description (e.g., Hauser–Feshbach theory, with optional width-fluctuation corrections), in which the cross section averaged over many overlapping resonances is expressed in terms of transmission coefficients and level densities. This requires the local density of CN resonances to be high enough that ensemble averages (e.g., a Gaussian Orthogonal Ensemble of the nuclear Hamiltonian, Porter–Thomas distributed widths) are meaningful.

Problem. Develop a quantitative reaction-theoretic framework for the intermediate regime in which neither (i) nor (ii) is applicable: that is, when the local density of compound-nucleus resonances is too small for a purely statistical (ensemble-averaged) treatment to be accurate, yet the number of resonances within the relevant energy window is too large for an explicit resonance-by-resonance parameterization. The framework should:

1. Provide a calculational scheme for energy-averaged and/or fluctuation cross sections that interpolates between the discrete R-matrix limit and the statistical Hauser–Feshbach limit;
2. Apply in particular to reactions on light nuclei and on nuclei far from the valley of stability, where this intermediate regime is generic;
3. Specify how level densities, partial-width distributions, channel correlations, and intermediate (doorway) structures are to be modeled when the number of contributing resonances is finite but not small;
4. Yield predictions whose accuracy can be tested against measured cross sections and resonance data.

Background. The Hauser–Feshbach statistical model describes a compound-nuclear reaction a + A → C* → b + B as proceeding through a long-lived compound nucleus C* whose decay is independent of its mode of formation (Bohr's hypothesis). In this framework the cross section for an entrance channel α to an exit channel β at total angular momentum J and parity π is written, schematically, as

   σ_{αβ}(E) = Σ_{J,π} g_J · (T_α^{Jπ}(E) · T_β^{Jπ}(E) / Σ_γ T_γ^{Jπ}(E)) · W_{αβ}^{Jπ}(E),

where T_c^{Jπ} are channel transmission coefficients (typically obtained from optical-model potentials, γ-ray strength functions, and nuclear level densities), the sum Σ_γ runs over all open decay channels, g_J is a spin statistical factor, and W_{αβ}^{Jπ} is a width-fluctuation correction factor that accounts for the non-vanishing correlation between entrance and exit widths in the Gaussian-orthogonal-ensemble limit of compound-nucleus resonances. Modern reaction codes implementing this formalism rely on the following standing assumptions:

  (i) Bohr's independence hypothesis (formation and decay are statistically independent);
  (ii) statistical-ensemble (GOE-type) modeling of compound-nucleus resonance amplitudes;
  (iii) prescribed phenomenological inputs for nuclear level densities ρ(E,J,π), photon strength functions f_{XL}(E_γ), and optical-model transmission coefficients;
  (iv) absence (or perturbative treatment) of pre-equilibrium and direct-semidirect contributions, of multi-step compound processes, and of non-statistical effects such as Porter–Thomas deviations, parity dependence, isospin mixing, and channel coupling to collective excitations.

Problem. Identify and formulate, in mathematically precise terms, the theoretical extensions of the Hauser–Feshbach framework — including but not limited to: (a) systematic treatment of width-fluctuation corrections beyond the Moldauer/HRTW approximations, (b) breakdown of Bohr's independence hypothesis at intermediate excitation energies, (c) coupling of compound, pre-equilibrium and direct reaction amplitudes in a unified projection-operator (Feshbach) formalism, (d) energy-, spin-, and parity-dependent level density models incorporating shell, pairing, and collective effects, and (e) photon strength functions including low-energy enhancements and M1 scissors/upbend modes — that must be implemented in compound-nuclear reaction codes, and rigorously test which of the assumptions (i)–(iv) above are valid in given energy/mass regimes, in order to obtain reliable and comprehensive predictions of compound-nuclear reaction observables (cross sections, angular distributions, and spectra) across the nuclear chart.

Consider the oblique, thread-like cool structures observed in solar prominences along the magnetic field lines of the large arches that emit at 304 Å (the He II resonance line, dominated by plasma at temperatures of order 10^4.5–10^5 K). These threads exhibit rapid plane-of-sky motions and apparent counter-streaming (oppositely directed flows along neighboring field lines).

Determine which of the following physical mechanisms is responsible for the observed fast motions of cool material along these oblique threads:

(a) Rapid radiative cooling and condensation of EUV-emitting (∼10^6 K) coronal plasma onto the field lines, with the resulting cool condensations sliding under gravity and pressure gradients;

(b) Large-amplitude longitudinal oscillations of the cool prominence material along dipped magnetic field lines, in which material trapped in different magnetic dips moves to one side or the other and produces an apparent counter-streaming pattern when integrated along the line of sight;

(c) Large-scale siphon flows driven by pressure differences between the two footpoints of long magnetic flux tubes, anchored in regions of unequal magnetic field strength, producing net mass transport from one end of the prominence to the other.

Identify, on the basis of observational and theoretical evidence (flow speeds, periodicities, spatial coherence, persistence, and footpoint conditions), which of (a), (b), or (c) is the dominant cause of the rapid oblique-thread flows in the large 304 Å arches, or give a quantitative criterion that decides between them.

In the standard ΛCDM cosmological framework, gas inside dark-matter halos is expected to acquire angular momentum through tidal torques and to subsequently cool and collapse to form rotationally supported disk galaxies. In practice, fully cosmological hydrodynamical simulations have historically produced disks that are too compact and bulges that are too massive: excessive early cooling of gas into small subhalos, followed by mergers, transfers a substantial fraction of the baryonic angular momentum to the surrounding dark matter (the so-called "angular momentum catastrophe"), yielding stellar systems whose specific angular momentum is much lower than that observed in real spiral galaxies.

Formulate and demonstrate a self-consistent prescription for baryonic physics (gas cooling, star formation, and stellar/AGN feedback) within a fully cosmological hydrodynamical simulation that, starting from cosmological initial conditions in a ΛCDM universe, produces at redshift z = 0 a Milky-Way-mass galaxy (halo mass ~10^12 M☉) which is:

(i) rotationally supported, with a disk circular velocity dominating over velocity dispersion (V/σ ≫ 1) throughout the stellar disk;
(ii) endowed with a thin, extended, high–angular-momentum stellar disk whose specific angular momentum j★ matches the observed Fall–Romanowsky relation for late-type spirals;
(iii) characterized by a small bulge-to-total stellar mass ratio (B/T ≲ 0.2), consistent with observed Sb–Sc spirals;
(iv) free from the angular-momentum catastrophe, in the sense that the net transfer of specific angular momentum from baryons to dark matter during assembly is small enough to leave the disk's j★ within observational scatter.

Specify quantitatively the physical mechanisms (e.g., feedback efficiency, coupling to the interstellar medium, suppression of early star formation in low-mass progenitors) responsible for retaining baryonic angular momentum, and show that the resulting galaxy population reproduces the observed thin-disk fraction.

A two-player bimatrix game is specified by a pair of real m×n payoff matrices (A, B), where the row player chooses a mixed strategy x ∈ Δ_m and the column player chooses y ∈ Δ_n (here Δ_k denotes the simplex of probability vectors in R^k). The pair (x, y) is a Nash equilibrium (NE) if x^T A y ≥ x'^T A y for all x' ∈ Δ_m and x^T B y ≥ x^T B y' for all y' ∈ Δ_n. Following Kannan and Theobald, define the rank of the game (A, B) to be rank(A + B). It is known that:
- rank-0 games (zero-sum games, B = -A) admit polynomial-time NE computation via linear programming;
- rank-1 games admit polynomial-time NE computation;
- for every fixed rank ≥ 3, computing a Nash equilibrium is PPAD-hard.

Question: Does there exist an algorithm that, given a bimatrix game (A, B) with rational entries and rank(A + B) = 2, computes a Nash equilibrium in time polynomial in the bit-size of the input? Equivalently, is the rank-2 case of bimatrix Nash equilibrium computation in P, or is it computationally intractable (e.g., PPAD-hard)?

Consider charged-current quasi-elastic-like (CCQE-like) neutrino-nucleus scattering, in which an incoming neutrino interacts with a bound nucleon inside a nuclear target and produces an outgoing charged lepton together with one or more outgoing nucleons (and possibly additional hadrons from final-state interactions). Existing theoretical models — for instance those incorporating random-phase approximation (RPA) corrections and multi-nucleon (np-nh) excitations — successfully reproduce inclusive observables such as the double-differential cross section in the outgoing charged-lepton kinetic energy and scattering angle, where one integrates over all possible hadronic final states.

Devise a theoretical framework for CCQE-like neutrino-nucleus interactions that:

1. Reproduces the measured inclusive outgoing charged-lepton kinematic distributions (including contributions from one-body and multi-nucleon mechanisms);
2. Simultaneously assigns, in a quantum-mechanically and kinematically consistent way, an exclusive hadronic final state to each event, specifying the multiplicity of outgoing protons (and neutrons) together with their individual momenta and angles;
3. Properly accounts for nuclear effects (Pauli blocking, Fermi motion, nucleon–nucleon correlations, and final-state interactions inside the residual nucleus);
4. Provides predictions detailed enough to be tested against exclusive measurements from fine-grained tracking detectors and liquid-argon time projection chambers that resolve outgoing proton tracks.

Specifically, construct (or prove the existence of) such a model and demonstrate its consistency between the inclusive lepton-side predictions and the exclusive hadronic-side observables (proton multiplicities and kinematic distributions) for CCQE-like events on nuclear targets such as carbon and argon.

Consider ab initio nuclear many-body calculations of the lepton–nucleus electroweak response that employ a realistic nucleon–nucleon interaction (such as the Argonne v18 potential) together with three-nucleon forces, with the nuclear ground and low-lying excited states obtained by quantum Green's Function Monte Carlo (GFMC) techniques. These calculations are intrinsically nonrelativistic and, owing to their computational cost, are presently feasible only for light nuclei (mass number A ≤ 12). For accelerator-based neutrino experiments, the charged-current quasi-elastic (CCQE) process ν_ℓ + A → ℓ^- + X involves momentum transfers |q| and energy transfers ω that can be comparable to or exceed the nucleon mass, so that relativistic kinematics and dynamics become essential.

Develop a theoretical framework that extends, or systematically connects, GFMC-type ab initio nuclear calculations of inclusive electroweak response functions to the relativistic regime of momentum and energy transfers relevant to accelerator neutrino beams, in such a way that:
(i) the high-quality treatment of nuclear correlations, two-body currents, and nuclear ground-state structure provided by GFMC is preserved;
(ii) relativistic kinematics for the struck nucleon(s) and the hadronic final state is incorporated consistently;
(iii) the resulting predictions for inclusive CCQE cross sections on nuclei of experimental interest are well-defined and can be compared with measured neutrino–nucleus data.

Specify the theoretical construction (e.g., choice of reference frame, treatment of the recoiling residual system, matching to relativistic current operators) and quantify the regime of (|q|, ω) in which the extension is justified.

Consider charged-current quasi-elastic-like (CCQE-like) neutrino–nucleus scattering events, in which an incoming neutrino of unknown energy E_ν interacts with a bound nucleon inside a target nucleus, producing an outgoing charged lepton of measured four-momentum p_ℓ and a hadronic final state that is only partially observable. The interaction may proceed via single-nucleon quasi-elastic scattering, or via scattering on short-range correlated nucleon pairs (so-called 2p2h or meson-exchange-current processes), in which case both nucleons must be returned on-shell, effectively shifting the kinematics. The incident neutrino energy is drawn from a flux distribution Φ(E_ν) which itself carries appreciable uncertainty. Detectors have finite energy thresholds for hadrons, limited or no neutron detection, and the initial-state nucleons exhibit Fermi motion characterized by a nuclear spectral function S(p, E).

Develop a procedure for reconstructing E_ν on an event-by-event basis (or, equivalently, a probabilistic mapping from observed final-state quantities to the true E_ν) that:

(i) takes as input only the observable lepton kinematics together with whatever subset of hadronic final-state particles exceeds detection thresholds;
(ii) properly accounts for Fermi motion via a specified spectral function, contributions from scattering on correlated nucleon pairs, finite hadronic detection thresholds, and the inability to measure final-state neutrons;
(iii) propagates the flux uncertainty in Φ(E_ν) and the model dependence of multinucleon (2p2h) contributions into a quantified bias and variance on the reconstructed energy;
(iv) minimizes, in a precise and stated sense (e.g., minimum mean-squared error or minimum bias subject to a coverage constraint), the systematic distortion of the reconstructed E_ν spectrum used in neutrino oscillation analyses.

Specify the mathematical form of the reconstruction map, prove or characterize its bias and variance properties, and give bounds on the residual model dependence.

Let $n \ge 2$ and let $F_n$ denote the free group of rank $n$ with free basis $\{x_1, \dots, x_n\}$. Let $F_n'$ be a second copy of $F_n$ with free basis $\{y_1, \dots, y_n\}$, and form the direct product $G = F_n \times F_n'$. The standard generating system of $G$ is $S_0 = (x_1, \dots, x_n, y_1, \dots, y_n)$, viewed as an ordered $2n$-tuple in $G^{2n}$.

Two ordered generating $k$-tuples $(g_1, \dots, g_k)$ and $(h_1, \dots, h_k)$ of $G$ are called Nielsen equivalent if one can be transformed into the other by a finite sequence of elementary Nielsen transformations, where the elementary Nielsen transformations on a tuple $(g_1, \dots, g_k)$ are:
(i) replacing some $g_i$ by $g_i^{-1}$;
(ii) permuting the entries of the tuple;
(iii) replacing some $g_i$ by $g_i g_j$ for some $j \ne i$ (the other entries being unchanged).

Question: Is every generating system of $G$ of cardinality $2n$ (i.e., every ordered $2n$-tuple of elements of $G$ that generates $G$) Nielsen equivalent to the standard generating system $S_0$?

Let G be a finitely generated group with finite generating set A, and let A* denote the set of finite words over A ∪ A^{-1}. A rational structure for G is a regular language L ⊆ A* that surjects onto G under the natural evaluation map A* → G. The structure (G, L) is called automatic if L is regular and, for every a ∈ A ∪ A^{-1} ∪ {1}, the "right-multiplier" relation
  R_a = { (u, v) ∈ L × L : u·a =_G v }
is a synchronously regular language over the product alphabet (using a padding symbol). The structure (G, L) is called biautomatic if, in addition to all R_a being regular, the corresponding "left-multiplier" relations
  L_a = { (u, v) ∈ L × L : a·u =_G v }
are also synchronously regular for every a ∈ A ∪ A^{-1} ∪ {1}. A group G is automatic (resp. biautomatic) if it admits some automatic (resp. biautomatic) structure with respect to some finite generating set.

Question: Does every automatic group admit a biautomatic structure? Equivalently, is every automatic group biautomatic?

Consider an isolated rotating neutron star whose angular frequency $\Omega(t)$ evolves under an external electromagnetic-type braking torque and an internal moment of inertia $I(\Omega;\,\mathrm{EOS},M,B,\ldots)$ that depends on rotation-induced stellar deformation, the (unknown) dense-matter equation of state, the stellar mass $M$, the composition of the core, and a time-dependent magnetic field $B(t)$. The angular momentum balance reads

$$ rac{d}{dt} igl(I(\Omega)\,\Omega igr) = -K(\Omega,B,	heta,\ldots)\,\Omega^{\,n_{m tor}},$$

where $K$ encodes the torque mechanism (e.g. magnetic dipole, plasma-filled magnetosphere, multipolar or wind torques, with inclination angle $	heta$ and possibly time-varying $B$), and $n_{m tor}$ is the intrinsic torque exponent. The observed braking index is defined by

$$n_{m obs} \;=\;  rac{\Omega\,\ddot\Omega}{\dot\Omega^{2}}.$$

Problem. Construct a single unified model of $\Omega(t)$ that simultaneously incorporates:
(i) low-frequency regimes where $n_{m obs}$ is governed by the torque law $K(\Omega,B,	heta,\ldots)$;
(ii) high-frequency regimes where deviations of $n_{m obs}$ from the torque-only value are dominated by the rotation dependence $dI/d\Omega$ arising from centrifugal deformation;
(iii) a family of physically admissible equations of state for neutron-star matter and the resulting mass dependence $I=I(\Omega;M,\mathrm{EOS})$;
(iv) uncertain interior composition (e.g. nucleonic, hyperonic, quark matter) and core microphysics (superfluidity, superconductivity, vortex pinning);
(v) magnetic-field evolution, including possible decay, growth, or geometric reorientation.

Determine, within this unified framework, the explicit functional form

$$n_{m obs} \;=\; \mathcal{F}\! igl(\Omega,\dot\Omega,\,I,I',I'';\,K,\partial_\Omega K,\partial_B K;\,\dot B,\dot	heta,\ldots igr),$$

and identify which combinations of the underlying microphysical parameters (EOS stiffness, mass, composition indicators, magnetic-field decay timescale, torque exponents) are constrained by a measurement of $n_{m obs}$ for a given pulsar. Specifically, characterize the inverse problem: given measured values $\{\Omega,\dot\Omega,\ddot\Omega\}$ (and where available $\dddot\Omega$) for a population of isolated pulsars spanning a wide range of spin frequencies, determine the largest set of microphysical parameters that can be uniquely or jointly bounded, and exhibit the model that achieves this.

Let $K$ be an arbitrary (abstract) field and let $A \in K^{n \times n}$ be an $n \times n$ matrix given as a black box: the only access to $A$ is through a routine that, on input a vector $v \in K^n$, returns the matrix-vector product $Av \in K^n$. Each such call is counted as one black-box call. Apart from black-box calls, all other arithmetic is performed in $K$, and intermediate storage is measured in number of field elements held simultaneously.

Design an algorithm (deterministic, Las Vegas, or Monte Carlo randomized) that computes the characteristic polynomial $\chi_A(x) = \det(xI - A) \in K[x]$ of $A$ within all of the following resource bounds:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $O(n)$ black-box calls to the matrix-vector multiplication routine for $A$;
\item $n^2 (\log n)^{O(1)}$ arithmetic operations in $K$;
\item $O(n)$ field elements of intermediate storage.
\end{enumerate}
Equivalently, decide whether such an algorithm exists.

Consider a discrete dynamical system specified by a state space $X$, a (possibly stochastic) update rule $F : X \to X$, and, when relevant, a parameter space $\Theta$ indexing a family $\{F_\theta\}_{\theta \in \Theta}$ of such rules. A perturbation is a map $\pi : X \to X$ that locally modifies a state (e.g., flipping a single component in a Boolean network). For an initial state $x \in X$ and a perturbation $\pi$, let $D_t(x,\pi)$ denote a chosen distance, at time $t$, between the unperturbed trajectory $\{F^k(x)\}$ and the perturbed trajectory $\{F^k(\pi(x))\}$, and let $\lambda(x,\pi) = \lim_{t\to\infty} \tfrac{1}{t}\log D_t(x,\pi)$ (or an analogous sensitivity measure) when the limit exists.

Different ensembles $\mathcal{E}$ over which one averages $\lambda$ yield different notions of "criticality":
(a) the uniform ensemble over all initial conditions $x \in X$;
(b) the ensemble of reachable states (states lying on some forward orbit from a designated initial set);
(c) the ensemble supported on a single attractor of $F$;
(d) the ensemble obtained by mixing over all attractors of $F$ with prescribed weights (e.g., basin-of-attraction sizes);
(e) ensembles over the parameter space $\Theta$, possibly combined with any of (a)–(d).

A system (or family) is called ordered, critical, or disordered according to whether $\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{E}}[\lambda] < 0$, $= 0$, or $> 0$, respectively.

Problem: Formulate a single, precise definition of a "critical dynamical system" that explicitly specifies (i) the class of admissible perturbations $\pi$, (ii) the ensemble $\mathcal{E}$ used for averaging, and (iii) the quantitative measure of perturbation effect, in such a way that the resulting trichotomy ordered/critical/disordered is invariant under natural equivalences of dynamical systems (e.g., relabeling of states, restriction to the reachable set when the transient is irrelevant) and reduces consistently to known criteria in benchmark cases such as random Boolean networks at the Derrida annealed-approximation phase transition. Determine the relations among the definitions obtained from ensembles (a)–(e), and identify those choices for which classification of a given system as ordered, critical, or disordered is independent of the choice within that class.

Consider a dynamical system S coupled to an external environment E through an interaction channel that allows exchange of information, energy, or matter. Suppose S is endowed with three (possibly intertwined) mechanisms of change: (i) evolution, modeled as stochastic variation and selection of the parameters or rules governing the dynamics of S on long timescales; (ii) adaptation, modeled as parameter adjustment driven by feedback from the current state of S and its interaction with E on intermediate timescales; and (iii) learning, modeled as updating of an internal representation that S maintains of regularities in E on short timescales. Say that S evolves toward a critical regime if, under the joint action of (i)–(iii), the long-time distribution of its operating parameters concentrates on a set where the system exhibits scale-invariant fluctuations (e.g., power-law distributed avalanche sizes, diverging susceptibility, or order-parameter fluctuations consistent with a continuous phase transition).

Problem. Identify general, mathematically precise conditions on:
(a) the structure of the environment E (its statistics, stationarity, correlations, and complexity);
(b) the system–environment coupling (its strength, topology, and the openness of S to flows from E);
(c) the joint dynamics of evolution, adaptation, and learning, including their relative timescales and how they share information,
under which the coupled system (S, E) provably drives S toward a critical regime in the sense above. Furthermore, give a rigorous explanation of the mechanism: characterize which feature of (a)–(c) is responsible for the emergence of criticality, and decide whether criticality requires all three of evolution, adaptation, and learning, or whether a strict subset suffices.

Consider an axisymmetric, vertically-integrated protoplanetary disc model in which a planet of mass M_p orbits at radius r_p in a gas disc of surface density Σ_g(r,t), temperature T(r), and turbulent viscosity parameter α. The planet opens a gap whose depth and width are determined by M_p, α, and the local disc aspect ratio h/r. Embedded in the gas is a population of dust grains described by a size distribution n(a, r, t) (with a the grain radius), evolving under the coupled processes of:

(i) radial drift driven by gas pressure gradients, with drift velocity v_drift(a, r) depending on the Stokes number St(a, r) and ∂P/∂r;
(ii) turbulent and differential-drift induced collisions producing coagulation up to a fragmentation barrier a_frag(r) set by a fragmentation velocity v_frag and α;
(iii) fragmentation cascades that replenish small grains;
(iv) opacity κ_ν(a) that, combined with the dust column, determines the emergent spectral energy distribution (SED) and millimetre-wavelength flux F_mm.

The transition-disc SED constraint requires that the small-grain (a ≲ a few μm) population in an inner region r < r_cav be reduced sufficiently to suppress near-infrared excess, while the millimetre flux F_mm and resolved mm emission morphology agree with observations of transition discs and the configuration persists on a timescale comparable to the disc lifetime τ_disc ∼ 10^6–10^7 yr.

Question. Determine whether there exists a non-empty region of the parameter space (M_p, r_p, α, Σ_g, h/r, v_frag, dust-to-gas ratio, κ_ν) consistent with realistic protoplanetary disc conditions for which the coupled drift–coagulation–fragmentation evolution of n(a, r, t), with self-consistent opacities, simultaneously:

(a) converts enough small dust into larger particles inside or near the planet-induced gap to reproduce the observed near-infrared deficit of transition-disc SEDs;
(b) does not over- or under-produce the observed millimetre continuum flux and spatial distribution; and
(c) maintains conditions (a) and (b) over a duration comparable to τ_disc despite ongoing radial drift, fragmentation, and possible replenishment of the small-grain population.

Provide either an explicit characterization of such a viable region of parameter space or a proof that no such region exists.

For positive integers $n_1<n_2<\dots<n_N$, define the GCD sum
$$S(n_1,\dots,n_N)=\sum_{k,\ell=1}^{N}\frac{\gcd(n_k,n_\ell)}{\sqrt{n_k n_\ell}}.$$
It is known that estimates for the maximum of $S$ over all choices of $N$ distinct positive integers (and weighted variants thereof) can be combined with Soundararajan's resonance method to produce lower bounds on the extreme values of the Riemann zeta function $\zeta(s)$ on the critical line, e.g.
$$\max_{T^{1/2}\le t\le T}\bigl|\zeta(\tfrac12+it)\bigr|\ \ge\ \exp\!\left((c+o(1))\sqrt{\frac{\log T\,\log\log\log T}{\log\log T}}\right)$$
for an explicit constant $c>0$.

Problem. Develop further quantitative and conceptual links between GCD-sum estimates of the above type (and their natural variants, including weighted GCD sums on multiplicative subsets and on Beurling generalized integers) and the analytic behaviour of $\zeta(s)$. In particular, find new applications of GCD-sum bounds that improve known results on:
(i) extreme values of $|\zeta(\tfrac12+it)|$ and of $\arg\zeta(\tfrac12+it)$ on intervals $[T,2T]$;
(ii) large values of $|\zeta(\sigma+it)|$ for fixed $\sigma\in(\tfrac12,1)$;
(iii) moments and resonance-type lower bounds for $\zeta$ and related $L$-functions;
and conversely identify intrinsic features of $\zeta$ that force, or are forced by, the known sharp asymptotics for $\sup S(n_1,\dots,n_N)$.

Consider an even–even atomic nucleus described in the Generator Coordinate Method (GCM) framework, in which collective states are written as superpositions

  |Ψ⟩ = ∫ f(β) |Φ(β)⟩ dβ,

where {|Φ(β)⟩} is a family of mean-field (Hartree–Fock–Bogoliubov or constrained Hartree–Fock) Slater-determinant-type generator states parameterized by the axial quadrupole deformation parameter β, and f(β) is the collective weight function. Define the norm overlap kernel

  N(β, β′) = ⟨Φ(β) | Φ(β′)⟩,

and define the deformation-coordinate coherence length Δβ as a measure of the width in |β − β′| over which N(β, β′) decreases from 1 to a fixed small value (so that two generator states whose deformations differ by less than Δβ are essentially indistinguishable, while those differing by more than Δβ are essentially orthogonal).

Develop a systematic, quantitative prescription that:

(a) provides a precise definition of Δβ from the overlap kernel N(β, β′) (for instance via the curvature of N at β′ = β, or via the spectrum of the norm operator), valid for a generic mean-field family {|Φ(β)⟩};

(b) computes Δβ explicitly for transitional and well-deformed even–even nuclei (in particular, for rare-earth isotopes near neutron number N = 98);

(c) compares Δβ with the spatial extent in β of GCM collective wave functions f(β) obtained after Hill–Wheeler–Griffin diagonalization, and uses this comparison to decide, for a given nucleus, whether the β-vibrational collective wave function can resolve zero nodes, one node, or two or more nodes in β;

(d) thereby explains, from first principles within mean-field/GCM theory, the qualitative pattern in which transitional nuclei support at most a one-node β vibration while well-deformed nuclei do not support any genuine β-vibrational excitation.

Consider an obliquely rotating, magnetized neutron star: a perfectly conducting star rotating with angular velocity Ω about an axis that is inclined at a nonzero angle α to its magnetic dipole axis, embedded in a relativistic electron–positron (and possibly ion) plasma that fills its magnetosphere and outflows as a relativistic wind. The rotation generates an inductive electric field E_ind = −(Ω×r)×B/c (in the non-corotating, observer frame), and Maxwell's equations include the displacement current ∂E/∂t.

Construct a self-consistent global model — i.e., a solution of the coupled system consisting of Maxwell's equations and the relativistic plasma equations of motion (with appropriate kinetic or fluid closure including pair creation) — for the electromagnetic field (E, B) and the plasma (charge density ρ, current density J, particle distribution functions) throughout the entire pulsar magnetosphere and wind, such that the model:

(a) is globally consistent across the closed-field region, the open-field region, the light cylinder (radius r_L = c/Ω), and the asymptotic wind zone, without assuming rigid corotation of the plasma with the star;
(b) explicitly accounts for the inductive electric field and the displacement current;
(c) admits localized regions ("gaps") in which the component of E parallel to B is nonzero (E_∥ ≠ 0), allowing particle acceleration and pair production, while remaining consistent with the global field and current structure;
(d) determines the locations and properties of these accelerating regions, the global current circuit, and the spin-down torque, without relying on the approximations (force-free, ideal MHD, vacuum, or strict corotation) whose individual limitations preclude a complete description.

Provide such a global model, or prove that it exists and characterize it; in particular, identify the locations within the magnetosphere and wind where charged particles are accelerated.

A pulsar is a rapidly rotating, strongly magnetized neutron star whose corotating magnetosphere is filled with a relativistic electron–positron (and possibly ion) plasma generated by pair cascades, and which emits highly polarized, extremely bright, narrow-band radio pulses whose brightness temperatures (often exceeding 10^25–10^30 K) require a coherent emission process rather than incoherent synchrotron-type radiation. Candidate coherent mechanisms that have been proposed include: (i) coherent curvature radiation by charged bunches or solitons moving along curved magnetic field lines; (ii) relativistic plasma maser/instability mechanisms (e.g., free-electron maser, anomalous-Doppler cyclotron-Cherenkov, or linear-acceleration emission); and (iii) various two-stream / parametric / turbulence-driven instabilities producing escaping electromagnetic modes.

Problem. Identify the specific coherent radio-emission mechanism that operates in pulsars and demonstrate that it is consistent, across the observed pulsar population (canonical pulsars, millisecond pulsars, magnetars, etc.), with all of the following simultaneously:
1. a physically motivated location of the source region within the magnetosphere (e.g., near the polar cap, in the outer gap, or at the light cylinder);
2. realistic local plasma parameters (multiplicity, Lorentz factor distribution, magnetic field strength, plasma frequency);
3. the inferred relativistic particle distribution functions, including any required beam/bunch/soliton structures and their stability;
4. the coherence requirements implied by the observed brightness temperatures;
5. the observed radio frequency range (roughly 100 MHz to a few GHz) and the form of the spectrum;
6. the observed polarization behavior, including the high degrees of linear polarization, orthogonal polarization modes, and characteristic position-angle swings.

Provide such a mechanism, with quantitative predictions derivable from first principles, or prove that no single mechanism among the existing candidates can simultaneously satisfy all of (1)–(6).

Background and setup. A vertex operator algebra (VOA) is a quadruple $(V,Y,\mathbf{1},\omega)$ where $V=\bigoplus_{n\in\mathbb{Z}}V_{(n)}$ is a $\mathbb{Z}$-graded vector space (with $\dim V_{(n)}<\infty$ and $V_{(n)}=0$ for $n$ sufficiently negative), $\mathbf{1}\in V_{(0)}$ is the vacuum, $\omega\in V_{(2)}$ is the conformal vector, and $Y(\cdot,x)\colon V\to(\mathrm{End}\,V)[[x,x^{-1}]]$ is the vertex operator map, satisfying the standard Jacobi identity, vacuum, creation, $L(-1)$-derivative, and Virasoro axioms.

A meromorphic open-string vertex algebra (MOSVA) is a $\mathbb{Z}$-graded vector space $V$ equipped with a vertex operator map $Y_V\colon V\otimes V\to V((x))$, a vacuum $\mathbf{1}$ and a conformal element $\omega$, satisfying the vacuum property, the $L(-1)$-derivative property, the Virasoro relations, and a weak associativity (rationality) axiom, but where the commutativity (skew-symmetry) of vertex operators is not required. Every VOA is in particular a MOSVA. A $V$-bimodule $W$ for a MOSVA $V$ is a $\mathbb{Z}$-graded vector space equipped with left and right vertex operator maps $Y^L_W\colon V\otimes W\to W((x))$ and $Y^R_W\colon W\otimes V\to W((x))$ compatible with the MOSVA structure (vacuum, $L(-1)$-derivative, weak associativities).

For a MOSVA $V$ and a $V$-bimodule $W$, there is a Hochschild-like cohomology $\widehat{H}^{n}_{\infty}(V,W)$ defined for each $n\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}$, where $n$-cochains are certain $W$-valued maps of $n$ variables in suitable spaces of formal/analytic series satisfying composability and convergence conditions, and the differential is the analogue of the Hochschild coboundary built from the left and right actions of $V$ on $W$.

Class of VOAs considered. Let $V$ be one of the following VOAs, viewed canonically as a MOSVA:
(a) the affine VOA $L_{\widehat{\mathfrak{g}}}(k,0)$ associated to a Wess–Zumino–Witten model at positive integer level $k$, for $\mathfrak{g}$ a finite-dimensional simple Lie algebra;
(b) a minimal model VOA $L(c_{p,q},0)$ of the Virasoro algebra at central charge $c_{p,q}=1-6(p-q)^2/(pq)$ with coprime integers $p,q\ge 2$;
(c) a lattice VOA $V_L$ associated to a positive-definite even lattice $L$;
(d) the moonshine module VOA $V^{\natural}$.

Question. Is it true that for every such $V$, every $V$-bimodule $W$ (in the MOSVA sense), and every positive integer $n$,
$$\widehat{H}^{n}_{\infty}(V,W)=0\,?$$

Let $V = \bigoplus_{n \in \mathbb{Z}} V_{(n)}$ be a vertex operator algebra with vertex operator map $Y : V \otimes V \to V[[x, x^{-1}]]$ and vacuum $\mathbf{1}$. Assume:

(i) $V_{(0)} = \mathbb{C}\mathbf{1}$ and $V_{(n)} = 0$ for $n < 0$;
(ii) the contragredient module $V'$ is isomorphic to $V$ as a $V$-module;
(iii) $V$ is $C_2$-cofinite, i.e., $\dim V / C_2(V) < \infty$, where $C_2(V) = \mathrm{span}\{\mathrm{Res}_x\, x^{-2} Y(u,x) v : u, v \in V\}$;
(iv) every grading-restricted generalized $V$-module is completely reducible.

Let $G$ be a finite group of automorphisms of $V$. For each $g \in G$, consider the category of grading-restricted generalized $g$-twisted $V$-modules, and let $\mathcal{C}_G = \bigoplus_{g \in G} \mathcal{C}_g$ be the direct sum of these categories over all $g \in G$.

Question: Does $\mathcal{C}_G$ carry the structure of a $G$-crossed (braided) tensor category in the sense of Turaev, with the $g$-twisted modules forming the $g$-graded component, the tensor product given by twisted fusion (sending $\mathcal{C}_g \times \mathcal{C}_h$ to $\mathcal{C}_{gh}$), and a $G$-action permuting the homogeneous components?

Let $V = \bigoplus_{n \ge 0} V_n$ be a vertex operator algebra over $\mathbb{C}$ satisfying the following three conditions:

(1) the central charge of $V$ equals $24$;
(2) every irreducible $V$-module is isomorphic to $V$ itself (i.e., $V$ is holomorphic);
(3) every $V$-module is completely reducible (i.e., $V$ is rational).

The homogeneous subspace $V_1$ of weight $1$ carries a Lie algebra structure given by the bracket $[a,b] = a_{(0)}b$, where $a_{(0)}$ denotes the zeroth mode of the vertex operator $Y(a,z)$.

Prove or disprove: any such $V$ is determined, up to isomorphism of vertex operator algebras, by the isomorphism class of the Lie algebra $V_1$. Equivalently, if $V$ and $V'$ both satisfy (1)–(3) and $V_1 \cong V'_1$ as Lie algebras, then $V \cong V'$ as vertex operator algebras.

Let X be a nonempty set and let d : X × X → [0, ∞) be a function (a "distance") satisfying the following properties:

(i) d(x, y) ≥ 0 for all x, y ∈ X, and d(x, y) + d(y, x) = 0 if and only if x = y;
(ii) d(x, y) = d(y, x) for all x, y ∈ X (symmetry);
(iii) (Fréchet / F-condition) for every ε > 0 there exists δ = δ(ε) > 0 such that, for all x, y, z ∈ X, the inequalities d(x, y) ≤ δ and d(y, z) ≤ δ imply d(x, z) ≤ ε.

A pair (X, d) satisfying (i)–(iii) is called a complete F-symmetric space if, in addition, every Cauchy sequence converges, where:
 • a sequence {x_n} ⊆ X is Cauchy when lim_{n,m → ∞} d(x_n, x_m) = 0;
 • {x_n} converges to x ∈ X when lim_{n → ∞} d(x, x_n) = 0.

Let g : X → X be a contraction, i.e., there exists a constant λ ∈ [0, 1) such that
 d(g(x), g(y)) ≤ λ · d(x, y) for all x, y ∈ X.

Question: Must g have a fixed point in X?

Let $M$ be a compact $8$-dimensional manifold with holonomy contained in $\mathrm{Spin}(7)$. Associated to $M$ one has:

(a) the Spin(7) elliptic genus $\mathrm{Ell}_M(\tau,\alpha)$, a two-variable modular-type invariant of $M$;

(b) the Shatashvili–Vafa Spin(7) superconformal vertex algebra $\mathcal{SV}^{\mathrm{Spin}(7)}$, whose irreducible unitary representations have well-defined characters (conjecturally given by certain mock modular forms);

(c) the chiral de Rham complex $\Omega^{\mathrm{ch}}_M$ of $M$, a sheaf of vertex superalgebras on $M$, together with a conjectural embedding of $\mathcal{SV}^{\mathrm{Spin}(7)}$ into the vertex algebra of global sections $C^\infty(M,\Omega^{\mathrm{ch}}_M)$ that is compatible with the Spin(7) structure;

(d) a distinguished odd element $Q^-_0$ acting on $C^\infty(M,\Omega^{\mathrm{ch}}_M)$, whose cohomology $H\big(C^\infty(M,\Omega^{\mathrm{ch}}_M),Q^-_0\big)$ inherits an action of $\mathcal{SV}^{\mathrm{Spin}(7)}$ and whose graded character recovers $\mathrm{Ell}_M(\tau,\alpha)$.

Question. Is the decomposition of $\mathrm{Ell}_M(\tau,\alpha)$ into characters of $\mathcal{SV}^{\mathrm{Spin}(7)}$ induced by a decomposition of the cohomology $H\big(C^\infty(M,\Omega^{\mathrm{ch}}_M),Q^-_0\big)$ into irreducible $\mathcal{SV}^{\mathrm{Spin}(7)}$-representations? Equivalently, do the multiplicity spaces appearing in the character decomposition of $\mathrm{Ell}_M(\tau,\alpha)$ arise as the multiplicity spaces of irreducible $\mathcal{SV}^{\mathrm{Spin}(7)}$-summands of this $Q^-_0$-cohomology?

Consider ultra-relativistic Pb–Pb collisions at LHC energies. Hadron yields measured at midrapidity are commonly described by a statistical (thermal) hadronization model in which the abundances of hadron species are fixed at a chemical freeze-out characterized by a temperature T_chem, a baryochemical potential μ_B, and a fireball volume V. The proton-to-pion ratio (p+p̄)/(π⁺+π⁻) measured at √s_NN = 2.76 TeV is found to lie significantly below the prediction obtained from a thermal fit using the value of T_chem extracted from pre-LHC data. Lowering T_chem by roughly 10 MeV brings the proton-to-pion ratio closer to the data, but creates tension with measured multi-strange hyperon abundances (Λ, Ξ, Ω), which favor the higher T_chem. Independently, ratios of short-lived resonances to their stable daughters (e.g. K*/K, ρ/π, Λ*/Λ) are well reproduced only by hadronic transport codes (such as EPOS or UrQMD) that include a finite hadronic rescattering phase between chemical and kinetic freeze-out, disfavoring single-temperature scenarios with T_chem = T_kin.

Identify and justify a physical mechanism, defined within a hydrodynamic-plus-hadronic-cascade framework, that simultaneously:
(a) reproduces the observed suppression of the (p+p̄)/(π⁺+π̄⁻) ratio at LHC;
(b) is consistent with the measured Λ, Ξ, and Ω hyperon yields without requiring a reduced T_chem;
(c) reproduces the measured short-lived resonance-to-stable ratios.

In particular, determine whether late baryon–antibaryon annihilation in the hadronic phase (equivalently, a late effective baryon freeze-out below T_chem driven by inelastic BB̄ → mesons reactions with insufficient back-reaction rate) can be uniquely distinguished from alternative explanations (e.g. flavor-dependent freeze-out, non-equilibrium pion chemical potentials, or modifications of the hadron mass spectrum) using currently available observables, or whether such alternatives remain phenomenologically degenerate.

An additive cube is a nonempty finite word $w = w_1 w_2 w_3$ over a finite alphabet $A \subset \mathbb{Z}$ such that the three factors $w_1, w_2, w_3$ have equal length and equal digit sums (i.e., $\sum_{i} w_1[i] = \sum_i w_2[i] = \sum_i w_3[i]$, where the letters are interpreted as integers). An infinite word $\mathbf{w} \in A^{\mathbb{N}}$ is called additive-cube-free if no factor of $\mathbf{w}$ is an additive cube.

Determine, for each of the following alphabets, whether there exists an infinite additive-cube-free word over it:

(a) $A = \{0, 1, 2, 3\}$;
(b) $A = \{0, 1, 4\}$;
(c) $A = \{0, 2, 5\}$.

Let a molecular system be described by a many-electron wavefunction Ψ on configuration space and an associated one-electron density ρ(r) on ℝ³. A topological atom Ω ⊂ ℝ³ is a region bounded by a surface S(Ω) of zero flux of ∇ρ, i.e. ∇ρ(r)·n(r) = 0 for all r ∈ S(Ω), where n is the outward unit normal. For a Coulombic molecular Hamiltonian, whose potential energy V is a sum of homogeneous functions of degree −1 in the nuclear–electronic coordinates, the subsystem virial theorem provides an origin-independent definition of the energy E(Ω) of the topological atom Ω, by exploiting Euler's homogeneity relation r·∇V = −V to combine the kinetic energy density T(Ω) and a virial contribution into an expression invariant under translations of the coordinate origin.

Problem. Suppose the molecular potential energy V is no longer purely Coulombic but takes one of the following forms:
(i) V = Σ_k V_k where each V_k is positively homogeneous of a (possibly different) degree α_k, with at least two distinct degrees occurring;
(ii) V is not expressible as a finite sum of positively homogeneous functions (for example, V arises from a finite-nucleus charge distribution model, or includes additional small magnetic-interaction terms that destroy homogeneity).

Provide a definition of the topological atom's energy E(Ω) such that:
(a) E(Ω) is independent of the choice of coordinate origin;
(b) E(Ω) reduces to the standard subsystem-virial-theorem definition when V is purely Coulombic;
(c) the total molecular energy decomposes additively as E = Σ_Ω E(Ω) over the disjoint topological atoms covering ℝ³;
(d) E(Ω) is expressible through local densities (kinetic energy density, potential energy density, and possibly stress-tensor-like fields) integrated over Ω together with surface contributions on S(Ω).

Either construct such a definition, or prove that no definition satisfying (a)–(d) can exist for the classes (i) and (ii) of non-Coulombic potentials.

Consider a sequential interaction between two agents, a learner $L$ and another agent $O$, taking place over discrete time steps $t = 1, 2, \dots$ in a stochastic environment specified by a state space $S$, joint action space $A_L \times A_O$, transition kernel $P(s' \mid s, a_L, a_O)$, and observation model from which $L$ observes a history $h_t = (s_1, a_{L,1}, a_{O,1}, \dots, s_t)$. The other agent $O$ chooses actions according to a behaviour policy $\pi_O^t : H \to \Delta(A_O)$ which is allowed to vary with $t$: for example, $\pi_O^t$ may be drawn from a finite or countable set of candidate policies $\{\pi^{(1)}, \pi^{(2)}, \dots\}$ with switches occurring at unknown times, or may evolve continuously through an adaptation/learning process unknown to $L$ (including the possibility that $O$ is itself a learner or a human whose strategy drifts).

The problem is to design, and rigorously analyze, a learning procedure for $L$ that simultaneously:
(i) identifies, at each time $t$, a model $\hat\pi_O^t$ of the current behaviour policy of $O$ from a hypothesis class $\Pi$ (which may itself need to be expanded online);
(ii) tracks changes in $\pi_O^t$ as they occur, detecting switches or drift with bounded delay;
(iii) predicts future actions $a_{O,t+1}, a_{O,t+2}, \dots$ with prediction error converging, in an appropriate sense, to that of an oracle that knows $\pi_O^t$;
under minimal stationarity assumptions on the sequence $(\pi_O^t)_{t \ge 1}$.

Formally: provide an algorithm together with a non-trivial regret or sample-complexity guarantee — for instance a bound on $\sum_{t=1}^T \mathbb{E}\,\mathrm{KL}\!\left(\pi_O^t(\cdot \mid h_t)\,\|\,\hat\pi_O^t(\cdot \mid h_t)\right)$ or on the cumulative loss of the resulting best response of $L$ — that holds whenever $(\pi_O^t)$ has bounded variation, a bounded number of switches, or arises from a known class of adaptive/learning dynamics, and which matches information-theoretic lower bounds for the corresponding non-stationary setting. Equivalently: characterize the classes of time-varying opponent behaviours for which such identification, tracking, and prediction are achievable, and exhibit matching upper and lower bounds.

Consider the Einstein vacuum equations Ric(g) = 0 on a 4-dimensional Lorentzian manifold (M, g). For parameters (m, a) with 0 ≤ |a| < m, the Kerr family g_{m,a} provides an explicit two-parameter family of stationary, axisymmetric, asymptotically flat solutions describing rotating black holes; the Schwarzschild solutions correspond to a = 0.

Given a vacuum initial data set (Σ, h, k) — that is, a 3-manifold Σ with a Riemannian metric h and a symmetric 2-tensor k satisfying the Einstein constraint equations — its maximal globally hyperbolic future development under the Einstein vacuum equations is uniquely determined.

Question: Prove that the Kerr family is nonlinearly asymptotically stable as a solution of the Einstein vacuum equations. More precisely, show that for every subextremal Kerr parameter pair (m_0, a_0) with |a_0| < m_0, there exists ε > 0 such that for any asymptotically flat vacuum initial data set (Σ, h, k) which is ε-close (in a suitable weighted Sobolev norm) to the induced data of Kerr (m_0, a_0) on a Cauchy hypersurface, the maximal future development:
(i) possesses a complete future null infinity;
(ii) admits a domain of outer communication that is bounded in the future by a smooth event horizon;
(iii) asymptotically settles down, in the exterior region, to a member g_{m_∞, a_∞} of the Kerr family with parameters (m_∞, a_∞) close to (m_0, a_0), with quantitative decay rates of the perturbation toward this final Kerr metric.

In general relativity, an evolving black hole's boundary is to be described in a quasi-local manner (i.e., without reference to global structure such as future null infinity or knowledge of the entire spacetime, in contrast to event horizons). Common candidate notions include:

- A trapped surface: a closed spacelike 2-surface S in a 4-dimensional Lorentzian spacetime (M,g) such that both null expansions θ_(ℓ) and θ_(n) of its two future-directed null normals are negative.
- A marginally trapped surface: a closed spacelike 2-surface on which θ_(ℓ) = 0 and θ_(n) ≤ 0.
- A dynamical horizon (Ashtekar–Krishnan): a smooth 3-dimensional spacelike submanifold H ⊂ M that admits a foliation by closed 2-surfaces on each of which one null expansion vanishes and the other is strictly negative.
- A trapping horizon (Hayward), a future outer trapping horizon, a marginally outer trapped tube (MOTT), etc.

Such candidates are known to suffer from defects: non-uniqueness of the foliation, dependence on the choice of slicing or null normal, lack of existence/uniqueness theorems for generic dynamical spacetimes, possible non-smoothness or jumps, and ambiguity of the "outermost" surface.

Problem. Formulate a mathematically rigorous and physically appropriate definition of a dynamical horizon for an evolving black hole in a general 4-dimensional Lorentzian spacetime (M,g) satisfying the Einstein equations, so that:

(i) the moving boundary of a black hole is characterized purely quasi-locally, without recourse to the global causal structure of (M,g);
(ii) the definition selects, in a canonical (foliation- and slicing-independent) way, a unique 3-dimensional hypersurface (or family thereof) representing the time-evolving black hole boundary;
(iii) the definition reduces to the standard event/Killing horizon notions in the stationary limit and to an isolated horizon in equilibrium;
(iv) existence, smoothness, and stability/uniqueness theorems can be established for physically reasonable evolving spacetimes (e.g., binary mergers, gravitational collapse);
(v) the standard laws of black hole mechanics (area increase, energy and angular momentum balance) hold along the hypersurface.

Provide such a definition, or prove that no definition satisfying (i)–(v) simultaneously can exist.

In semiclassical gravity, a black hole formed from gravitational collapse of matter in a pure quantum state is predicted by Hawking's calculation to evaporate by emitting thermal (mixed) radiation, eventually disappearing. This appears to map an initial pure state to a final mixed state, contradicting the unitary time evolution required by quantum mechanics, and seemingly destroying information about the initial state.

Provide a precise theoretical resolution of this paradox. Specifically, formulate a consistent framework that describes the entire process of black-hole formation and complete evaporation in such a way that:

(i) the global evolution from the initial infalling matter state to the final outgoing radiation state is unitary, so that pure states evolve to pure states;
(ii) all information about the initial state is in principle recoverable from the final state (no genuine loss of information);
(iii) the framework reproduces, to leading order in the appropriate semiclassical limit, Hawking's prediction of approximately thermal radiation at the Hawking temperature for black holes far from the endpoint of evaporation;
(iv) the framework is consistent with the equivalence principle for an infalling observer (i.e. no drama at the horizon for a sufficiently large black hole) or, alternatively, gives a principled explanation of which classical expectation must be modified and why.

Identify the underlying mechanism (e.g. subtle correlations in the Hawking radiation, non-locality, modifications to the near-horizon geometry, holographic encoding, or otherwise) by which information is preserved, and demonstrate mathematically that the proposed mechanism is compatible with quantum mechanics, general relativity in its domain of validity, and the observed entanglement-entropy behavior (Page curve) of the radiation.

Consider relativistic nucleus–nucleus (A–A) collisions over the full range of center-of-mass energies per nucleon pair √s_NN, spanning sub-threshold beam energies, the SPS regime (√s_NN of a few to ~17 GeV), the RHIC regime (up to 200 GeV), and the LHC regime (up to several TeV). For a strange hadron species h carrying strangeness |S|, define the strangeness enhancement factor by

   E_h(√s_NN) = ( Y^{AA}_h / ⟨N_part⟩ ) / ( Y^{pp}_h / 2 ),

where Y^{AA}_h is the mean yield of species h per A–A event, ⟨N_part⟩ is the mean number of participating nucleons in the A–A collision, and Y^{pp}_h is the mean yield of h in inelastic proton–proton collisions at the same √s_NN.

Determine the functional form of E_h(√s_NN) over the entire energy range, in particular for the rare multi-strange baryons and anti-baryons Ξ⁻, Ξ̄⁺, Ω⁻ and Ω̄⁺ (|S| = 2 and 3). Specifically, characterize:

(i) the behavior of E_h near and below the elementary strangeness production threshold;
(ii) the location and magnitude of any maximum of E_h as a function of √s_NN;
(iii) the high-energy limit of E_h at LHC energies;
(iv) the dependence of E_h on the strangeness content |S| of the hadron and the difference, if any, between particles and their antiparticles.

Background. A nonlocal differential equation is a functional equation for an unknown function u(x), x = (x_1,...,x_n), that involves, in addition to u and its partial derivatives up to some finite order, one or more nonlocal terms such as integrals of u over subsets of the domain, values of u at shifted points, or auxiliary potential variables defined implicitly through u. A typical example is an integro-differential equation of the form

   F( x, u, ∂u, ∂^2 u, ..., ∂^k u, ∫ K(x,y) u(y) dy ) = 0,

which can equivalently be reformulated in many analytically distinct ways, for instance:
 (a) as a single scalar nonlocal equation in u alone;
 (b) as an enlarged local system obtained by introducing one or more potential variables v_1,...,v_m together with auxiliary equations (e.g. ∂_x v = u, etc.) so that the original nonlocal term becomes a local function of (u, v_1,...,v_m) and their derivatives;
 (c) as a different local system obtained by a different choice of potential variables or by a change of dependent/independent variables;
 (d) as a differential-difference or differential-delay system, etc.

Classical Lie symmetry analysis applies most directly to local differential equations: one seeks vector fields whose prolongation leaves the equation invariant. When the equation is nonlocal, the resulting symmetry group can depend strongly on the chosen representation; some representations may admit a rich Lie point symmetry algebra while equivalent representations of the same underlying equation may admit only trivial Lie point symmetries (the missing symmetries appearing instead as nonlocal or potential symmetries).

Problem. Develop an a priori criterion that, given a nonlocal differential equation E together with a list of analytically equivalent reformulations R_1, R_2, ..., R_N (each being either a scalar nonlocal equation or a local system as above), determines, without first computing all of the corresponding symmetry algebras, which representation R_j yields the largest (or otherwise most useful) Lie point symmetry algebra for the purposes of symmetry calculations. Equivalently: identify intrinsic structural features of E and of the maps E ↔ R_j that predict the dimension and structure of the Lie point symmetry algebra of R_j, and use these features to single out an optimal representation in advance.

Let a system of differential equations (either ordinary or partial) be given, and let G be a Lie (point, contact, generalized, or nonclassical) symmetry group admitted by the system, where nonclassical symmetries are understood in the sense of Bluman–Cole: transformations leaving invariant the joint system consisting of the original equations together with the invariant surface conditions associated with the candidate vector fields. The task is to design, analyze, and further develop numerical discretization schemes (finite difference, finite element, finite volume, spectral, mesh-adaptive, or moving-frame based) on discrete grids such that the resulting discrete equations admit a discrete analogue of the symmetry group G acting on the lattice and dependent variables, in such a way that:

(i) every continuous symmetry (including nonclassical ones) of the differential system is inherited by the discrete scheme as an exact symmetry of the corresponding difference equations on the (possibly transformed) mesh;
(ii) the scheme is consistent with the differential system and convergent, with stability and accuracy properties at least comparable to standard non-invariant schemes;
(iii) group-invariant solutions, similarity reductions, and conservation laws stemming from the symmetries (including those obtained via nonclassical reductions) are reproduced exactly, or to higher order, by the numerical solutions.

Provide constructive algorithms achieving (i)–(iii), particularly for partial differential equations and for nonclassical symmetries, together with a convergence and error analysis framework that exploits the inherited symmetry structure.

Let $X$ be an infinite-dimensional complex Banach space (for example a separable complex Hilbert space, or a space such as $\ell^p(\mathbb{C})$ with $1 \le p \le \infty$), and let $f : U \to X$ be a holomorphic self-map defined on an open subset $U \subseteq X$ that is forward invariant under $f$. Consider the discrete dynamical system generated by iteration of $f$, with Fatou set $F(f)$ defined as the largest open subset of $U$ on which the family of iterates $\{f^{\circ n}\}_{n \ge 1}$ is locally equicontinuous (or, equivalently in suitable settings, locally normal), and Julia set $J(f) = U \setminus F(f)$.

Formulate a precise, intrinsic definition of "chaos" for such an infinite-dimensional holomorphic dynamical system $(X, f)$ that:

(a) reduces, when $X = \mathbb{C}^n$ for some finite $n \ge 1$ and $f$ is a holomorphic endomorphism, to a notion equivalent to the classical notion of chaos on the Julia set (e.g.\ in the sense of Devaney: topological transitivity, density of periodic points, and sensitive dependence on initial conditions);

(b) is preserved under holomorphic conjugation $f \mapsto \varphi^{-1} \circ f \circ \varphi$ by biholomorphisms $\varphi$ of $X$;

(c) accommodates the genuinely infinite-dimensional features of $X$ (in particular, the failure of local compactness and of Montel's theorem, the existence of unbounded linear operators with rich spectral behavior, and the distinction between norm, weak, and weak-$*$ topologies), in such a way that natural classes of examples — such as hypercyclic bounded linear operators on $X$, holomorphic perturbations thereof, and shifts on sequence spaces — are recognized as chaotic on appropriate invariant subsets.

Provide such a definition and verify properties (a), (b), and (c).

Let $(M,\omega,j)$ be a compact prequantizable Kähler manifold of complex dimension $2$, with prequantum line bundle $(L,h)\to M$ whose Chern curvature is $-i\omega$. For each integer $k\ge 1$, let $\mathcal H_k=H^0(M,L^{\otimes k})$ be the space of holomorphic sections, and let $\Pi_k:L^2(M,L^{\otimes k})\to\mathcal H_k$ denote the Bergman/Szegő projector. The Berezin–Toeplitz quantization of a smooth function $f\in C^\infty(M)$ is the family of operators $T_k(f)=\Pi_k\,M_f\,\Pi_k$ acting on $\mathcal H_k$, with semiclassical parameter $\hbar=1/k$.

Consider a completely integrable system $F=(f_1,f_2):M\to\mathbb R^2$ of two Poisson-commuting smooth functions, and assume that $m\in M$ is a non-degenerate critical point of focus–focus type, i.e. there exist symplectic coordinates $(x_1,y_1,x_2,y_2)$ near $m$ in which the Hessians of $f_1,f_2$ at $m$ span the Cartan subalgebra of focus–focus type, so that the singular fiber through $m$ is a pinched torus and the local model of the Lagrangian foliation is given by the Hamiltonians $q_1=x_1y_1+x_2y_2$, $q_2=x_1y_2-x_2y_1$.

Let $T_k(f_1)$ and $T_k(f_2)$ be the associated commuting Berezin–Toeplitz operators. Formulate explicit singular Bohr–Sommerfeld quantization conditions characterizing, modulo $O(k^{-N})$ for every $N$, the joint spectrum of $(T_k(f_1),T_k(f_2))$ in a fixed neighborhood of the critical value $F(m)\in\mathbb R^2$ as $k\to\infty$. The rules must:
(i) include the analogue of the regular Bohr–Sommerfeld action integrals along vanishing cycles of the focus–focus pinched torus, with all subprincipal and curvature corrections specific to the Berezin–Toeplitz setting;
(ii) account for quantum monodromy around the focus–focus value;
(iii) provide an asymptotic expansion in powers of $1/k$ to all orders for the joint eigenvalues.

Let $n \geq 2$. Consider the following three topological groups of holomorphic self-maps of $\mathbb{C}^n$, each endowed with the compact-open topology:

(a) $\mathrm{Aut}(\mathbb{C}^n)$, the group of biholomorphic automorphisms of $\mathbb{C}^n$;

(b) $\mathrm{Aut}_\omega(\mathbb{C}^n)$, the group of holomorphic automorphisms preserving the standard holomorphic volume form $\omega = dz_1 \wedge \cdots \wedge dz_n$;

(c) when $n = 2m$ is even, $\mathrm{Aut}_\sigma(\mathbb{C}^{2m})$, the group of holomorphic automorphisms preserving the standard holomorphic symplectic form $\sigma = \sum_{j=1}^{m} dz_j \wedge dz_{m+j}$.

For a self-map $f : \mathbb{C}^n \to \mathbb{C}^n$, say that:
- $f$ has a dense orbit if there exists $z \in \mathbb{C}^n$ such that the forward orbit $\{f^k(z) : k \geq 0\}$ is dense in $\mathbb{C}^n$;
- $f$ is chaotic (in the sense of Devaney) if it has a dense orbit and the set of its periodic points is dense in $\mathbb{C}^n$;
- a Borel probability measure $\mu$ on $\mathbb{C}^n$ is invariant under $f$ if $f_*\mu = \mu$, and ergodic if every $f$-invariant Borel set has $\mu$-measure $0$ or $1$.

Investigate, in each of the classes (a), (b), and (c), the following genericity questions: 
(1) Is the set of elements possessing a dense orbit residual (i.e., contains a dense $G_\delta$ set) in the corresponding group? 
(2) Is the set of chaotic elements residual in the corresponding group? 
(3) For a generic element $f$ in the corresponding group, describe the structure of $f$-invariant (resp. ergodic) Borel probability measures on $\mathbb{C}^n$.

Work in the complex projective space $\mathbb{P}^k$ with $k\geq 3$. A singular holomorphic foliation $\mathcal{F}$ on $\mathbb{P}^k$ of dimension $d$ (with $1<d<k$) is given, away from a singular set of codimension $\geq 2$, by an integrable system of holomorphic forms; its leaves are the maximal connected immersed holomorphic submanifolds of dimension $d$ tangent to $\mathcal{F}$ on the smooth locus.

Call $\mathcal{F}$ elementary if it admits a (possibly multivalued) meromorphic first integral of one of the following types:
(i) $\mathcal{F}$ is defined by a closed meromorphic $(k-d)$-form on $\mathbb{P}^k$;
(ii) $\mathcal{F}$ is defined by a meromorphic $(k-d)$-form $\omega$ such that $f\,\omega$ is closed for some meromorphic function (integrating factor) $f$;
(iii) $\mathcal{F}$ is a logarithmic foliation, i.e. defined by a form $\omega=\sum_i \lambda_i\, \frac{df_i}{f_i}\wedge(\text{...})$ producing a multivalued first integral of the form $\prod_i f_i^{\lambda_i}$ with $f_i$ homogeneous polynomials and $\lambda_i\in\mathbb{C}$.

Problem: Construct a non-elementary holomorphic foliation $\mathcal{F}$ on $\mathbb{P}^k$ whose leaves have dimension strictly greater than $1$ and such that no leaf of $\mathcal{F}$ contains any compact algebraic curve.

Among B-type main-sequence stars with effective temperatures in the range $T_{\mathrm{eff}} \approx 17000$–$25000$ K, three distinct chemical classes are observed to coexist:

1. He-strong stars, whose photospheric helium abundance is markedly enhanced relative to the cosmic value;
2. He-weak stars, whose photospheric helium abundance is markedly depleted relative to the cosmic value;
3. Chemically normal B-type stars, with approximately solar/cosmic helium abundance.

Provide a physical explanation for why all three helium-abundance classes are simultaneously present in this same narrow effective-temperature interval on the main sequence. In particular, identify the mechanism (or combination of mechanisms — e.g. atomic diffusion, radiative levitation, gravitational settling, stellar winds/mass loss, magnetic field geometry and strength, rotation, or pulsational mixing) that determines which of the three helium-abundance classes a given B-type main-sequence star in this temperature range occupies, and predict from stellar parameters (mass, rotation rate, magnetic field strength and topology, mass-loss rate, age) the resulting class membership.

Let $D \subset \mathbb{C}$ be a doubly-connected domain with boundary components $\alpha$ and $\beta$, and let $z_0, z_1 \in D$. Let $A, B \in \mathbb{R}$ be given real numbers. Consider the class $\mathcal{F}$ of all functions $u \colon D \to [-\infty, \infty)$ such that:
\begin{itemize}
\item $u$ is subharmonic in $D$;
\item $u$ extends continuously to $\alpha \cup \beta$ with boundary values $u \equiv A$ on $\alpha$ and $u \equiv B$ on $\beta$;
\item there exists a curve $\gamma \subset \overline{D}$ joining $z_1$ to a point of $\alpha$ along which $u \le 0$.
\end{itemize}
Determine
\[
\sup_{u \in \mathcal{F}} u(z_0),
\]
and identify the extremal function (if one exists).

Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ denote the open unit disc. Let:

- $S$ be the class of functions $f(z)=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} a_n z^n$ that are analytic and univalent in $\mathbb{D}$ with $a_1=1$ (the standard normalised class);
- $S^{*}$ be the class of all univalent analytic functions $f(z)=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} a_n z^n$ on $\mathbb{D}$, without normalisation;
- $K$ be the class of close-to-convex functions in $\mathbb{D}$ (functions $f$ analytic in $\mathbb{D}$ for which there exists a convex univalent $g$ such that $\operatorname{Re}(f'(z)/g'(z))>0$ in $\mathbb{D}$);
- $C$ be the class of convex univalent functions in $\mathbb{D}$;
- $N$ be the Nevanlinna class of analytic functions of bounded characteristic in $\mathbb{D}$;
- $N^{+}$ be the Smirnov class.

Given two classes $A, B$ of analytic functions on $\mathbb{D}$, a sequence $(\lambda_n)_{n\ge 1}$ of complex numbers is called a coefficient multiplier sequence from $A$ into $B$ if, for every $f(z)=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} a_n z^n \in A$, the function $(\lambda \ast f)(z) := \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \lambda_n a_n z^n$ belongs to $B$. Denote the set of all such sequences by $M(A,B)$.

Provide an intrinsic characterisation (analogous to the result that $(\lambda_n)\in M(C,C)$ if and only if $\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \lambda_n z^n \in C$) of the following multiplier classes:

(a) $M(S,S)$, the multipliers from $S$ into itself with the normalisation $a_1=1$;
(b) $M(S^{*}, S^{*})$, the multipliers from the class of all univalent functions into itself without normalisation;
(c) $M(K,K)$, the multipliers of the class of close-to-convex functions into itself;
(d) $M(S,C)$, the multipliers from $S$ into the class $C$ of convex functions;
(e) $M(N,N)$, the multipliers from the Nevanlinna class into itself; and the analogous problem $M(N^{+},N^{+})$.

In each case, give a necessary and sufficient condition on the sequence $(\lambda_n)$.

Let $\mathbb{D} = \{z \in \mathbb{C} : |z| < 1\}$ denote the open unit disc. For analytic functions $f, g : \mathbb{D} \to \mathbb{C}$, we say $f$ is subordinate to $g$, written $f \prec g$, if there exists an analytic function $\omega : \mathbb{D} \to \mathbb{D}$ with $\omega(0) = 0$ such that $f(z) = g(\omega(z))$ for all $z \in \mathbb{D}$. Given an analytic function 
$$g(z) = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} B_n z^n, \qquad z \in \mathbb{D},$$ 
let $S_g = \{ f : \mathbb{D} \to \mathbb{C} \text{ analytic} : f \prec g\}$, and let $\overline{\mathrm{co}}(S_g)$ denote the closed convex hull of $S_g$ in the topology of locally uniform convergence on $\mathbb{D}$. The rotations $g_t(z) := g(z e^{it})$, $0 \le t < 2\pi$, always belong to $S_g$ and are extreme points of $\overline{\mathrm{co}}(S_g)$ in known examples such as
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] $g(z) = \left(\frac{1+cz}{1-z}\right)^{\alpha}$ with $|c| \le 1$ and $\alpha \ge 1$;
\item[(b)] $g(z) = \exp\!\left(\frac{1+z}{1-z}\right)$;
\item[(c)] $g(z) = h\!\left(\frac{1+z}{1-z}\right)$, where $h$ is a univalent quadratic polynomial on the half-plane $\{w : \mathrm{Re}\, w > 0\}$.
\end{itemize}

Determine general conditions on $g$ which ensure that the only extreme points of $\overline{\mathrm{co}}(S_g)$ are the rotations $\{g(z e^{it}) : 0 \le t < 2\pi\}$. In particular, does this conclusion hold whenever the Taylor coefficients $B_n$ of $g$ are positive and increasing in a suitably regular manner?

Let $\Sigma$ denote the class of functions
$$F(z)=z+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} b_n z^{-n}$$
that are meromorphic and univalent on the exterior disk $\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|>1\}$. For $F\in\Sigma$, let
$$G(w)=w+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} c_n w^{-n}$$
denote the inverse of $F$ (defined and univalent on the exterior of some disk of sufficiently large radius, with $c_n$ the Laurent coefficients of $F^{-1}$ at infinity).

Determine, for every integer $n\ge 1$, the exact (sharp) values of
$$\sup_{F\in\Sigma}|b_n|\qquad\text{and}\qquad \sup_{F\in\Sigma}|c_n|.$$

The following sharp bounds are already known and need not be reproved:
\begin{itemize}
\item $|b_1|\le 1$ and $|c_1|\le 1$ (area theorem);
\item $|b_2|\le \tfrac{2}{3}$ and $|c_2|\le \tfrac{2}{3}$;
\item $|b_3|\le \tfrac{1}{2}+e^{-6}$;
\item $|c_3|\le 1$.
\end{itemize}
The task is to find the sharp value of $\sup_{F\in\Sigma}|b_n|$ and of $\sup_{F\in\Sigma}|c_n|$ for the remaining cases (in particular, for all $n\ge 4$, and for $|b_n|$ when $n\ge 3$ beyond the partial bound above).

Let $F(z_1, z_2)$ be a real-valued function defined on $\{(z_1,z_2)\in\mathbb{C}^2 : |z_1|\le 1,\ |z_2|\le 1\}$ except on the set $\{z_1=z_2,\ |z_1|=|z_2|=1\}$. Assume that:

(i) $F$ is plurisubharmonic on the open bidisc $\{|z_1|<1,\ |z_2|<1\}$;

(ii) $F(z_1,z_2) \le \log|z_1-z_2|$ wherever the left-hand side is defined;

(iii) $F(z_1,z_2) \le 0$ for all $(z_1,z_2)$ with $|z_1|=|z_2|=1$ and $z_1\ne z_2$.

Does it necessarily follow that $F(z_1,z_2) \le 0$ for all $(z_1,z_2)$ with $|z_1|<1$ and $|z_2|<1$?

Let $D \subseteq \mathbb{C}$ be a domain and let $f \colon D \to \mathbb{C}$ be a continuous function. For $z \in D$ and $r>0$ small enough that the closed disk $\overline{B(z,r)} \subset D$, consider the contour integral
$$I(z,r) \;=\; \int_{|\zeta-z|=r} f(\zeta)\, d\zeta,$$
taken over the positively oriented circle of radius $r$ centred at $z$.

Suppose that one of the following two hypotheses holds:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] there exists a function $r \colon D \to (0,\infty)$ such that $I(z,r) = 0$ for every $z \in D$ and every $r$ with $0 < r \le r(z)$;
\item[(ii)] (weaker) for every $z \in D$,
$$\lim_{r \to 0^+} r^{-2}\, I(z,r) \;=\; 0.$$
\end{itemize}

Does it follow, under hypothesis (i), or under the weaker hypothesis (ii), that $f$ is analytic (holomorphic) in $D$?

Let $\mathbb{D} = \{z \in \mathbb{C} : |z| < 1\}$ denote the open unit disk, and let $E \subset \mathbb{C}$ be an arbitrary subset. A map $F : E \times \mathbb{D} \to \mathbb{C}$ is called a holomorphic motion of $E$ if it satisfies:

(a) for each fixed $z \in \mathbb{D}$, the map $w \mapsto F(w, z)$ is injective on $E$;
(b) for each fixed $w \in E$, the map $z \mapsto F(w, z)$ is analytic on $\mathbb{D}$;
(c) $F(w, 0) = w$ for every $w \in E$.

Question: Does every such $F$ extend to a map $G : \mathbb{C} \times \mathbb{D} \to \mathbb{C}$ which itself satisfies conditions (a), (b), (c) (with $E$ replaced by $\mathbb{C}$) and agrees with $F$ on $E \times \mathbb{D}$?

A symmetry-protected topological (SPT) phase in (d+1) spacetime dimensions is a gapped phase of matter with a global symmetry G that is short-range entangled in the bulk and whose nontriviality is detected by anomalous boundary degrees of freedom of dimension d. In (2+1) dimensions, boundary conformal field theory (BCFT) techniques have been developed to diagnose and characterize SPT phases: by placing a (1+1)-dimensional CFT on a half-space or interval and classifying the conformal boundary conditions (together with their associated boundary states, g-functions, and anomaly inflow data), one can detect the bulk SPT class and its symmetry anomalies.

Formulate and develop a higher-dimensional analogue of this program. Specifically, construct a framework of boundary/defect conformal field theory in d ≥ 3 spacetime dimensions in which:

(i) one defines the appropriate notion of conformal boundary conditions for a (3+1)-dimensional bulk theory whose boundary supports a (2+1)-dimensional surface theory;
(ii) the framework accommodates surface theories that are either gapless conformal field theories or that carry intrinsic topological order (i.e. are described by topological quantum field theories with anyonic excitations);
(iii) the boundary/defect data (boundary central charges, anomaly coefficients, defect operator content, etc.) provide invariants that distinguish distinct (3+1)-dimensional SPT phases protected by a given symmetry group G, including time-reversal and unitary internal symmetries.

Prove or disprove the existence of such a framework, and if it exists, give explicit invariants and computations characterizing nontrivial (3+1)-dimensional bosonic and fermionic SPT phases via these higher-dimensional BCFT methods.

A quasi Riemann surface is a structure generalizing the notion of a Riemann surface in which one is given a collection of "quasi holomorphic curves" each carrying a one-complex-dimensional analytic structure, together with compatibility data on overlaps that allow one to formulate a Cauchy–Riemann-type equation globally. On an ordinary Riemann surface, complex analysis provides: holomorphic and meromorphic functions and differentials, the Cauchy–Riemann equations, contour integration and the residue theorem, the Riemann–Roch theorem, period matrices, theta functions, uniformization, and moduli of complex structures.

Develop the analogous theory of complex analysis on a quasi Riemann surface. Specifically, formulate and establish on a quasi Riemann surface the natural analogues of:
(a) holomorphic and meromorphic functions and differential forms;
(b) the Cauchy–Riemann equations and Cauchy-type integral formulas;
(c) a residue calculus and period structure;
(d) a Riemann–Roch-type theorem relating dimensions of spaces of meromorphic sections to a topological/analytic invariant;
(e) moduli of quasi complex structures and an analogue of uniformization.

Provide definitions and proofs that parallel the classical theory on genuine Riemann surfaces, and identify the precise sense in which classical complex analysis is recovered when the quasi Riemann surface reduces to an ordinary Riemann surface.

Consider a supervised machine-learning training procedure that produces a trained model M as the output of an algorithm T applied to inputs (S, D, H, θ0, r), where:
- S denotes the model structure (architecture, layer definitions, loss function, optimizer family);
- D denotes the training dataset (including its exact contents, ordering, and any preprocessing pipeline);
- H denotes the training system and hyperparameters (software stack with versions, hardware, numerical precision, batch size, learning-rate schedule, number of epochs, etc.);
- θ0 denotes the initial values of all learnable parameters;
- r denotes the random seeds used by every stochastic component of training (data shuffling, augmentation, dropout, parallel non-determinism, etc.).

Because the learned parameters of M cannot be specified a priori by a designer, the only available form of "specification" for M is an indirect one: a record R and a re-execution process P such that running P on R reproduces M.

Problem. Determine a minimal set of artifacts R = (S, D, H, θ0, r, …) and a re-execution process P with the following property: for any two independent executions of P on R, the resulting trained models M1 and M2 are bitwise identical (or, in a relaxed formulation, functionally equivalent on every input). In particular, identify what additional information beyond {model structure, training data, training system, hyperparameters, initial parameters, random seeds} — for example concerning floating-point non-associativity, concurrent thread scheduling, GPU kernel non-determinism, library versions, and operating-system level state — must be recorded, and what constraints must be imposed on P, in order to guarantee reproducibility of the training of a safety-critical machine-learning model.

The six Painlevé equations P_I, P_II, P_III, P_IV, P_V, P_VI are the canonical second-order nonlinear ordinary differential equations (in the complex domain) whose solutions have no movable critical singularities other than poles. Their general solutions, called Painlevé transcendents, define new transcendental functions. Unlike classical second-order linear equations (e.g. Bessel's or the hypergeometric equation), the Painlevé equations do not admit two canonical "representative" solutions: each equation possesses a rich variety of qualitatively distinct solution families, including rational solutions, algebraic solutions, classical (Riccati-type) solutions expressible via special functions, tronquée and tritronquée solutions, solutions with prescribed asymptotic behavior on rays, solutions characterized by their monodromy/Stokes data, and one- or two-parameter families distinguished by connection formulae.

Problem. Develop a coherent, standard system of notation for Painlevé transcendents that:
(a) names individual solutions of each Painlevé equation P_J (J = I, …, VI) for given parameter values;
(b) accommodates and clearly distinguishes the wide variety of qualitatively different solution families (rational, algebraic, classical/special-function, tronquée, tritronquée, real on the real line, with prescribed asymptotics, with prescribed monodromy data, etc.);
(c) is unambiguous, extendable, and suitable for adoption as a community-wide convention analogous to the standard notation used for classical special functions.

Consider ultrahigh-energy cosmic rays (UHECRs), defined as cosmic-ray particles with energies above the spectral feature known as the "ankle" (approximately 5 EeV = 5 × 10^18 eV). These particles, after propagating through intergalactic space subject to energy losses (e.g., photopion production, photodisintegration, adiabatic expansion) and deflections by Galactic and extragalactic magnetic fields, are observed at Earth with measured energy E, mass-composition indicator (e.g., depth of shower maximum X_max), and arrival direction n̂ on the celestial sphere.

Let {G_i} denote a catalog of candidate extragalactic host galaxies (or galaxy types, e.g., starburst galaxies, active galactic nuclei, radio galaxies) within the GZK propagation horizon, parameterized by redshift z, luminosity L in some relevant band, and sky position. Let R(z) denote the comoving redshift evolution of the UHECR emissivity associated with a candidate population.

The problem is the following:

(a) Determine which subclass(es) of galaxies host the accelerators responsible for UHECRs above the ankle; equivalently, identify the source population P such that the observed joint distribution of (E, X_max, n̂) of UHECRs is consistent with emission from P (after modeling propagation and magnetic deflection) and inconsistent with emission from competing populations.

(b) Specify the combination of observational and modeling ingredients sufficient to upgrade a statistical correlation between UHECR arrival directions and a catalog into a credible source identification rather than a spurious correlation. In particular, determine quantitative requirements on:
  (i) full-sky exposure (uniform coverage of the entire celestial sphere, eliminating blind regions where a distinct source class could contribute);
  (ii) constraints on the redshift evolution R(z) of the UHECR production rate, enabling a tomographic discrimination of source populations;
  (iii) completeness of the candidate-source catalog up to the propagation horizon and down to a sufficiently low luminosity threshold L_min;
  (iv) joint statistical analyses simultaneously accounting for energy, mass composition, and arrival directions of the events.

Provide the identification in (a) and the quantitative thresholds in (b) such that the hypothesis "population P sources the observed UHECRs above the ankle" can be established at high statistical significance against well-defined alternatives.

Consider the dilute dipolar quantum Ising magnet modeled on a lattice of sites occupied with probability x by magnetic Ho³⁺ ions (treated as effective two-level pseudospins σᵢ with Ising axis along the crystalline c-direction) and with probability 1−x by non-magnetic Y³⁺ ions. The microscopic effective Hamiltonian is taken to have the form

  H = −∑_{i<j} J^{zz}_{ij} σᵢ^z σⱼ^z + ∑_{i<j} (off-diagonal pseudospin couplings J^{αβ}_{ij} σᵢ^α σⱼ^β, α,β ∈ {x,y,z}, (α,β)≠(z,z)) − Γ ∑_i σᵢ^x + ∑_i A Iᵢ · Sᵢ + H_nuc,

where:
 • J^{zz}_{ij} are the longitudinal (effective Ising) couplings derived from projecting the magnetic dipole–dipole interaction onto the lowest crystal-field doublet of Ho³⁺;
 • the off-diagonal terms J^{αβ}_{ij} arise from virtual admixing of higher crystal-field levels and from the transverse components of the dipolar interaction;
 • Γ is an applied (or effective) transverse field;
 • A Iᵢ · Sᵢ is the on-site contactic hyperfine coupling between the Ho³⁺ electronic moment and its I = 7/2 nuclear spin, and H_nuc contains nuclear-only terms;
 • the sums run only over occupied (Ho³⁺) sites under quenched site dilution at concentration x.

Existing numerical investigations of this model in the highly dilute regime (small x) have been carried out using purely classical Monte Carlo on the longitudinal Ising part. Direct application of standard worldline/stochastic-series-expansion quantum Monte Carlo to the full Hamiltonian is obstructed by a sign problem produced jointly by the off-diagonal pseudospin couplings J^{αβ}_{ij} and by the hyperfine coupling A Iᵢ · Sᵢ.

Problem. Devise a numerical scheme for the highly dilute regime of the above model that simultaneously incorporates (i) the hyperfine interaction A Iᵢ · Sᵢ on every occupied site and (ii) the off-diagonal pseudospin couplings J^{αβ}_{ij}, while controlling or circumventing the resulting sign problem, and that scales to system sizes and temperatures sufficient for quantitative (not merely qualitative) comparison with experimental observables (such as susceptibilities, specific heat, and dynamic response) in dilute LiHo_xY_{1−x}F₄.

Consider cryptographic schemes whose security relies on algorithmic problems in non-commutative (nonabelian) groups, such as the conjugacy search problem, the word problem, the membership problem, or related decision/search problems in finitely presented groups (e.g., braid groups, polycyclic groups, Thompson's group, free products with amalgamation). For such schemes, several distinct notions of computational hardness can be used to assess security:

(a) practical hardness, measured by the empirical performance of the best known attacks on concrete instances;
(b) worst-case complexity, i.e., the asymptotic difficulty of the underlying algorithmic problem on its hardest instances;
(c) average-case complexity, i.e., the expected difficulty under a specified distribution on inputs;
(d) generic-case complexity, i.e., the difficulty of the problem on a subset of inputs of asymptotic density 1 with respect to a specified stratification of the input set;
(e) any other formally defined notion of hardness.

Problem. Determine which of these notions (or what alternative notion) provides the appropriate and rigorous foundation on which to base the security evaluation of cryptographic protocols built from non-commutative groups. Specifically:

1. Formulate a precise definition of "secure non-commutative cryptographic scheme" in terms of one or more of the above complexity notions.
2. Justify the chosen notion by exhibiting a class of non-commutative cryptographic protocols and a class of algorithmic group-theoretic problems for which the chosen complexity measure correctly predicts resistance to all known classes of attacks (length-based attacks, linear/representation attacks, quotient attacks, statistical attacks, etc.).
3. Show, conversely, that the alternative notions on the list are inadequate, by exhibiting either schemes that are hard in another sense yet broken in practice, or schemes that satisfy the proposed notion but fail under one of the other measures.

In semi-analytic models of galaxy formation, baryonic processes are coupled to dark-matter halo merger trees through prescriptions for (i) star formation (typically a Kennicutt–Schmidt-like law relating star-formation rate surface density to cold gas surface density above a threshold), (ii) supernova feedback (parameterized by an efficiency that reheats and/or ejects cold interstellar gas as a function of circular velocity or halo mass), (iii) radiative cooling of hot halo gas onto the central galaxy, and (iv) gas heating or expulsion by additional energetic processes. Calibrating such a model to reproduce the observed stellar mass function at redshift z = 0 over the full mass range — in particular its low-mass end (stellar masses below roughly 10^10 M_⊙) — currently requires very strong supernova feedback efficiencies. Standard implementations that adopt this strong feedback simultaneously fail one or more of the following observational constraints:

(a) the specific star-formation rates of low-mass galaxies at z = 0 are predicted too low;
(b) the predicted fraction of red (passive) low-mass galaxies is too high;
(c) the predicted stellar mass functions at intermediate redshifts 0.8 ≤ z ≤ 2.5 have amplitudes that are too high;
(d) the predicted galaxy two-point auto-correlation function is too high for galaxies with stellar masses below 6 × 10^10 M_⊙;
(e) the supernova feedback efficiency required exceeds what can be physically motivated from the available energy budget of core-collapse supernovae;
(f) modifications that alleviate (a)–(e) by weakening feedback or altering the star-formation law overpredict the cold gas (HI + H_2) content of low-mass galaxies.

Problem. Construct a physically motivated set of prescriptions for star formation, supernova feedback, hot-gas cooling, and gas heating/expulsion (possibly including a presently unidentified high-redshift gas-removal or gas-heating mechanism), implemented within a semi-analytic galaxy-formation model on a cosmological dark-matter merger tree, such that the model simultaneously matches:
- the observed z = 0 galaxy stellar mass function across all stellar masses, including the low-mass end;
- the observed specific star-formation rates of low-mass galaxies at z = 0;
- the observed red/blue (passive/active) fractions for low-mass galaxies;
- the observed stellar mass functions at redshifts in [0.8, 2.5];
- the observed galaxy auto-correlation function for galaxies with stellar mass below 6 × 10^10 M_⊙;
- the observed cold-gas mass function and gas-to-stellar-mass ratios of low-mass galaxies;
while keeping the supernova feedback efficiency within the physically allowed energy budget. Determine whether such a combination of prescriptions exists and, if so, exhibit one explicitly.

For positive integers n and k ≥ 2, let C_{2k} denote the cycle on 2k vertices, and let ex(n, C_{2k}) denote the maximum number of edges in a simple graph on n vertices that contains no subgraph isomorphic to C_{2k}. Prove or disprove the following: for every integer k ≥ 2, there exists a positive constant c = c(k) such that
ex(n, C_{2k}) ≥ c · n^{1+1/k}
for all sufficiently large n.

Astrophysical and cosmological observations (galactic rotation curves, gravitational lensing, the cosmic microwave background, and large-scale structure of the Universe) imply that a substantial fraction of the total mass of the Universe is nonluminous and non-baryonic, and searches for compact baryonic objects (such as MACHOs) have ruled them out as the full explanation of this missing mass. Any candidate constituent of this dark matter must satisfy:

(i) it is (almost) stable on the timescale of the age of the Universe (~1.4 × 10^{10} years), so that it has not decayed away;
(ii) it interacts only very weakly with ordinary (Standard Model) matter, so as to evade existing direct-detection experiments;
(iii) its cosmological abundance and clustering properties are consistent with the observed dark-matter density Ω_DM and with structure formation.

Identify the particle(s) or composite object(s) — necessarily lying beyond the Standard Model of particle physics — that constitute the dark matter, specifying their masses, spins, interaction couplings, and production mechanism in the early Universe, in a way compatible with all current astrophysical, cosmological, collider, and direct- and indirect-detection constraints.

Consider an N-electron quantum system with Hamiltonian H = H_0 + V_ext(t), where H_0 contains kinetic energy and pairwise Coulomb interactions and V_ext(t) is a weak, time-dependent external one-body perturbation. For p = 1, 2, ..., N, define the p-th reduced density matrix by partial trace, ρ_p(x_1,...,x_p; x'_1,...,x'_p; t) = (N!/(N-p)!) Tr_{p+1,...,N} ρ_N(t), where ρ_N(t) is the full density matrix evolving via i ∂_t ρ_N = [H, ρ_N]. The reduced density matrices satisfy the BBGKY hierarchy, in which the equation for ρ_p couples to ρ_{p+1} through the two-body interaction and is generally nonlinear when expressed via cluster (cumulant) decompositions ρ_2 = ρ_1 ∧ ρ_1 + c_2, ρ_3 = ρ_1 ∧ ρ_1 ∧ ρ_1 + 3 ρ_1 ∧ c_2 + c_3, etc.

Linearize the hierarchy around a stationary reference state ρ^{(0)}_p (e.g., the ground state) by writing ρ_p(t) = ρ^{(0)}_p + δρ_p(t) and retaining only terms of first order in δρ_p and in V_ext. The resulting equations of motion form the linear-response BBGKY hierarchy.

Develop a closed, well-posed truncation of this linear-response hierarchy at finite order p ≤ p_0 (for some small p_0, e.g. p_0 = 2 or 3) such that:

(i) the truncation defines a linear time-evolution generator L on the truncated set (δρ_1, ..., δρ_{p_0}) whose spectrum yields finite, physically meaningful neutral excitation energies (poles of the optical response);
(ii) basic conservation laws (particle number, total energy at the reference state, and, when applicable, the f-sum rule for the dipole response) are preserved;
(iii) the dynamics is free of the secular/divergent behavior known to afflict nonlinear truncations of the time-dependent BBGKY hierarchy, in the sense that ‖δρ_p(t)‖ remains bounded for all t ≥ 0 under bounded V_ext;
(iv) in suitable limits the scheme reduces to known linear-response approximations such as time-dependent Hartree–Fock (RPA) at p_0 = 1 and to second-RPA-type theories at p_0 = 2.

Construct such a truncation, prove (i)–(iv), and characterize the resulting optical absorption spectrum σ(ω) = -(ω/π) Im Tr[d̂ δρ_1(ω)] for a dipole operator d̂.

In relativistic heavy-ion collisions, the transverse geometry of the initial state at a fixed impact parameter is commonly characterized by a set of dimensionless eccentricity (initial-anisotropy) coefficients. For an event with energy- or entropy-density distribution ρ(x,y) in the transverse plane (recentered to its center of mass), the n-th order anisotropy is typically defined via the complex moment

   ε_n e^{i n Φ_n}  =  − ∫ r^{w(n)} e^{i n φ} ρ(r,φ) r dr dφ  /  ∫ r^{w(n)} ρ(r,φ) r dr dφ ,

but several inequivalent conventions are in use, differing in the radial weight w(n) (e.g. w(n)=n versus w(n)=2 or w(n)=3), in whether ρ is taken to be the participant-nucleon density, the binary-collision density, an entropy density, or an energy density, and in how the center of mass and event plane are defined. Glauber Monte Carlo implementations also differ in the nucleon size and shape, the inclusion of nucleon–nucleon correlations, the inelastic cross section parametrization, the treatment of wounded versus binary scaling, and the smearing of point-like sources into a continuous density. In experimental flow analyses, the observed Fourier harmonics v_n of the azimuthal particle distribution are extracted by a variety of methods (event-plane, two- and multi-particle cumulants, scalar product, Lee–Yang zeros, flow tensors), each with different sensitivities to flow fluctuations and nonflow correlations.

Problem. Formulate a single, mathematically precise, community-usable standard that simultaneously fixes:

(a) a canonical definition of the initial-anisotropy coefficients ε_n (specifying the density ρ, the radial weight w(n), the centering prescription, and the event-plane angle Φ_n);

(b) a canonical Glauber Monte Carlo specification (nucleon profile, correlations, cross section, source smearing, and participant/binary weighting);

(c) a canonical set of experimental flow observables and estimators v_n[method] together with the precise mathematical relationships between them and the underlying event-by-event distribution P(v_n, Ψ_n);

such that the resulting mapping ε_n ↦ v_n computed in any hydrodynamic or transport model implemented to the standard yields results that are directly and quantitatively comparable across models and across experimental measurements. Determine whether such a standardization is achievable, and if so, exhibit one explicit choice and prove that under it the leading model-to-model and method-to-method ambiguities in v_n/ε_n are removed.

Consider a supermassive black hole (a compact massive object whose gravitational potential dominates dynamics within its sphere of influence) residing at the center of a galaxy, surrounded by a circumnuclear region of gas and stars. Using observational constraints obtained from the Galactic Center of the Milky Way and from the nuclei of nearby galaxies, characterize:

(a) the modes and rates of gas accretion onto the central black hole, including the role of tidal disruption of gas clouds;

(b) the properties of star formation in the immediate vicinity of the black hole (the circumnuclear region), including initial mass function, spatial distribution, and triggering mechanisms;

(c) the quantitative relationship between black-hole feeding (gas inflow and accretion), feedback (radiative, mechanical, and outflow-driven energy and momentum injection into the surrounding medium), and ongoing or recent circumnuclear star formation;

(d) the influence of the central compact massive object on the stellar and gaseous dynamics of the surrounding nuclear region.

Provide a unified empirical and theoretical description that explains how feeding, feedback, and star formation are coupled in the central parsec-to-hundred-parsec environment of a supermassive black hole.

Consider an active galactic nucleus modeled as a central black hole of mass $M_{\bullet}$ and dimensionless spin $a_{\bullet}\in[0,1]$ accreting matter at a rate $\dot{M}$, with an associated Eddington ratio $\lambda = \dot{M} c^{2}/L_{\rm Edd}$ and radiative efficiency $\eta(\dot{M},a_{\bullet})$ defined so that the bolometric luminosity is $L_{\rm bol} = \eta\,\dot{M} c^{2}$. The accretion flow is threaded by a large-scale magnetic field of characteristic strength $B$ (or, equivalently, magnetic flux $\Phi$ through the horizon, with magnetically arrested disk parameter $\phi = \Phi/(\dot{M} r_{g}^{2} c)^{1/2}$, where $r_{g}=GM_{\bullet}/c^{2}$). The system may produce a relativistic jet of mechanical power $P_{\rm jet}$ and/or a wide-angle outflow of mechanical power $P_{\rm out}$.

Determine, quantitatively and as a function of the parameter set $(M_{\bullet}, a_{\bullet}, \dot{M}, B, \ldots)$:

(a) the explicit functional dependence relating $P_{\rm jet}$, $P_{\rm out}$, and $\eta$ to one another;

(b) a sharp criterion (a region in the parameter space spanned by $M_{\bullet}$, $a_{\bullet}$, $\lambda$, and $\phi$, plus any further physical parameters required) that decides whether jets are launched, whether outflows are launched, both, or neither;

(c) the relative roles of black-hole spin versus accretion rate versus magnetic flux in setting $P_{\rm jet}/L_{\rm bol}$ and $P_{\rm out}/L_{\rm bol}$.

In particular, identify the minimal complete set of physical parameters whose values fully determine the production and energetics of jets and outflows in active galactic nuclei.

Consider galaxies hosting active galactic nuclei (AGN), where an AGN is a compact, luminous central region powered by accretion onto a supermassive black hole, and star formation is the production of new stars within the host galaxy. Distinguish two spatial regimes of star formation: (i) galaxy-wide star formation occurring throughout the host disk and bulge, and (ii) nuclear star formation occurring in the circumnuclear region within roughly the central few hundred parsecs around the supermassive black hole. Consider both the low-redshift universe (z ≲ 0.1) and the high-redshift universe (z ≳ 1).

Determine the character of the relationship between AGN activity (measured, e.g., by bolometric or X-ray luminosity of the active nucleus) and star formation rate in each of these two spatial regimes and at each redshift range. In particular, decide whether the relation is best described as:
(a) coincidental — the two phenomena occur together by chance, driven by independent processes such as common gas availability without dynamical coupling;
(b) causal — one phenomenon directly triggers or quenches the other (e.g., AGN feedback suppressing or inducing star formation, or starburst-driven gas inflow fueling the AGN);
(c) self-regulated — the two phenomena mutually modulate each other through feedback loops involving gas inflow, accretion, and energy/momentum injection that maintain a quasi-equilibrium;
(d) some combination of the above, possibly differing between galaxy-wide and nuclear scales and between low- and high-redshift epochs.

Provide a characterization supported by observational and theoretical reasoning that distinguishes the galaxy-wide AGN/star-formation relation from the nuclear AGN/star-formation relation across cosmic time.

Consider the random-matrix-theory model of compound-nucleus scattering in which the scattering matrix S(E) is constructed from a Hamiltonian drawn from the Gaussian Orthogonal Ensemble (GOE) coupled to a finite number of open channels via fixed channel-coupling vectors. For incident energy E, write S_{ab}(E) for the channel-to-channel S-matrix elements and define the fluctuating cross sections σ_{ab}(E) ∝ |S_{ab}(E) − ⟨S_{ab}(E)⟩|^2.

The following GOE averages are known analytically:
(i) the two-point S-matrix correlator ⟨S_{ab}(E_1) S_{cd}^*(E_2)⟩ for arbitrary degree of resonance overlap;
(ii) selected three- and four-point S-matrix correlators;
(iii) the marginal probability distribution of an individual S-matrix element;
(iv) the complete joint probability distribution of all S-matrix elements, but only in the Ericson regime of strongly overlapping resonances (Γ ≫ D, where Γ is the mean resonance width and D the mean level spacing).

Problem. Derive practically usable closed-form GOE expressions for cross-section correlation functions
C_{ab,cd}(ε) = ⟨σ_{ab}(E) σ_{cd}(E+ε)⟩ − ⟨σ_{ab}(E)⟩ ⟨σ_{cd}(E+ε)⟩
that are valid outside the Ericson regime — in particular in the regime of isolated and weakly overlapping resonances (Γ ≲ D) — for arbitrary numbers of open channels and arbitrary transmission coefficients. More generally, obtain analytic expressions for higher cross-section correlations and for the complete joint distribution of S-matrix elements beyond the Ericson limit.

Consider the bootstrap algebraic multigrid (BAMG) framework for solving large sparse linear systems $A u = f$ arising from the discretization of partial differential equations on a grid or unstructured mesh. In BAMG, a multilevel hierarchy is constructed adaptively: a set of test vectors is generated by relaxation and used to define coarsening (selection of coarse degrees of freedom) and interpolation operators $P_\ell$ between successive levels, from which coarse operators $A_{\ell+1} = P_\ell^T A_\ell P_\ell$ are formed. In its standard form, the setup phase relies only on algebraic information available from the matrix $A$ and the computed test vectors.

Devise a systematic methodology that augments the bootstrap algebraic multigrid setup so that, whenever supplementary information about the underlying problem is available — in particular geometric information such as the spatial coordinates of degrees of freedom, mesh connectivity, element shape, anisotropy directions, coefficient variations, or boundary data — this information is used to:

(a) guide the selection of coarse variables (coarsening);
(b) determine the sparsity pattern and weights of the interpolation operators $P_\ell$;
(c) influence the choice of smoothers and the overall setup;

in such a way that the resulting multigrid hierarchy provably (or empirically, with quantifiable improvement) exploits the supplied geometric/problem information, yielding faster convergence and lower setup cost than the purely algebraic bootstrap procedure, while still reducing to the algebraic procedure when no extra information is provided.

A solar prominence is a region of cool, dense plasma (with temperatures of order 10^4 K and densities much higher than the ambient corona) that is suspended in the much hotter (of order 10^6 K) and more tenuous solar corona, supported against gravity by the magnetic field. The prominence-corona transition region (PCTR) is the thin interface in which the plasma temperature, density, ionization state, and emission properties change steeply between prominence values and coronal values.

Determine the physical structure of the PCTR. Specifically, give a quantitative description of:

(a) the geometry and topology of the interface (e.g., whether it forms a smooth sheath surrounding the cool prominence threads, or whether it is organized into many unresolved fine structures), together with characteristic length scales (thicknesses) along and across the magnetic field;

(b) the profiles of temperature, electron density, pressure, and ionization fraction across the interface, and the dominant physical processes (thermal conduction, radiative losses, enthalpy flux, ambipolar diffusion / ion-neutral decoupling, wave heating, turbulent mixing, non-equilibrium ionization) that set these profiles;

(c) the orientation of the local magnetic field with respect to the temperature gradient, and the resulting anisotropy of energy and mass transport;

(d) the dynamical state (steady vs. unsteady flows, oscillations, instabilities such as Rayleigh–Taylor or Kelvin–Helmholtz) and its observational signatures in EUV, UV, and optical spectral lines.

The goal is a self-consistent model of the PCTR, supported by observational diagnostics, that explains the simultaneous emission observed from chromospheric, transition-region, and coronal temperature lines associated with prominences.

Consider the standard astrophysical procedure for inferring the stellar mass of a galaxy by fitting its observed multi-band spectral energy distribution (SED) to synthetic spectra produced by a stellar population synthesis (SPS) model. The synthetic spectra are obtained by convolving stellar evolutionary tracks and stellar atmosphere libraries with an assumed initial mass function (IMF), a star formation history (SFH), a chemical-enrichment/metallicity prescription, a treatment of advanced evolutionary phases (in particular the thermally pulsing asymptotic giant branch, TP-AGB), a dust attenuation law and assumed dust–star geometry, a prescription for nebular emission lines, and possible additional physics such as stellar rotation and binary stellar evolution.

Quantify the true total systematic uncertainty (e.g. as a multiplicative factor or dex offset) on the inferred stellar mass M⋆ of an individual galaxy that arises from the joint freedom in:
(i) the choice of SPS code/model;
(ii) the treatment of the TP-AGB phase;
(iii) the assumed metallicity;
(iv) the assumed IMF (including potentially non-universal or unusual IMFs);
(v) the parametrization of the SFH;
(vi) the adopted dust attenuation law and dust geometry;
(vii) the treatment of nebular emission lines;
(viii) the inclusion of stellar rotation;
(ix) the inclusion of binary stellar evolution; and
(x) other physical ingredients that are currently missing or untested in SPS models ("unknown unknowns").

In particular, determine a defensible upper bound on the systematic error budget for SED-derived stellar masses that accounts not only for the variation among existing model choices ("known unknowns") but also for ingredients not yet incorporated or tested.

In hierarchical and hydrodynamical models of galaxy formation, the assembly of dark matter halos proceeds bottom-up: low-mass halos assemble most of their mass at early cosmic times. When galaxies are placed inside these halos via prescriptions for gas cooling, star formation, stellar/AGN feedback, and gas recycling/accretion, the resulting low-mass galaxy population exhibits the following systematic discrepancies with observations:

(i) the number densities of low- and intermediate-mass galaxies (stellar mass roughly 10^9 – 10^10.5 M_sun) are overpredicted at intermediate and high redshift;
(ii) low-mass galaxies in models are too old and too passive (their stellar populations form too early and their present-day star formation rates are too low);
(iii) the observed anti-correlation between specific star formation rate (sSFR ≡ SFR/M_*) and stellar mass M_* is not reproduced;
(iv) any successful prescription must decouple the late stellar-mass growth of low-mass galaxies from the early mass growth of their host halos, while not overproducing the reservoir of cold gas in these galaxies.

Problem: Identify and specify a physical mechanism — formulated in terms of stellar/supernova feedback, AGN feedback, gas recycling from previously ejected material, or modulation of gas accretion onto low-mass halos — that, when implemented in hierarchical semi-analytic models and/or hydrodynamical simulations of galaxy formation in a ΛCDM cosmology, simultaneously resolves (i)–(iv) without requiring ad hoc suppression of cooling or accretion, and without violating observational constraints on the cold gas content of low-mass galaxies.

Consider a polling system consisting of $N \ge 2$ queues $Q_1,\dots,Q_N$ served by a single server. Customers arrive at queue $Q_i$ according to independent Poisson processes with rates $\lambda_i$, requiring i.i.d. service times with finite mean. Whenever the server moves from one queue to another it incurs a (random, finite-mean) switch-over time. A polling system is specified by three ingredients:

(i) a service discipline at each queue (e.g., exhaustive, gated, $k$-limited, time-limited, Bernoulli, globally gated, etc.) prescribing how many customers are served during a visit;
(ii) a visit discipline / routing rule, which may be a fixed cyclic or periodic order, a polling-table order, or a dynamic rule in which the next queue to be served is chosen based on the current state rather than a pre-determined order;
(iii) a scheduling discipline within each queue (e.g., FCFS, LCFS, processor sharing, priority).

Scale all switch-over times by a common factor $s$ and let $s \to \infty$, keeping arrival rates and service-time distributions fixed (and the system stable in the appropriate sense). Say that the polling system exhibits a deterministic large-switch-over-time limit if, under suitable scaling, the cycle times converge to a deterministic limit and the (scaled) waiting-time distribution at each queue converges to a non-degenerate limit law (e.g., a uniform distribution) that depends on the disciplines only through a small number of explicit parameters (such as the exhaustiveness index of the service discipline).

Problem. Give a complete classification of the triples (service discipline, visit discipline, scheduling discipline) for which the polling system exhibits this deterministic large-switch-over-time limit. In particular, characterize all dynamic (non-pre-determined) visit orders and all service disciplines (including customer-limited and time-limited families) that give rise to such limit theorems, and identify the exact parameters of the disciplines that determine the limiting distributions.

Consider proton–proton collisions at center-of-mass energy √s = 7 TeV in the high-multiplicity regime, where event samples are typically selected by requiring a large number N_ch of reconstructed charged primary particles within a fixed pseudorapidity acceptance (e.g. |η| < 2.4) and above a transverse-momentum threshold (e.g. p_T > 0.25 GeV/c). In such samples, particles produced by hard parton scatterings (jets, mini-jets, multi-parton interactions) are mixed with particles produced by soft processes (underlying event, fragmentation of low-Q² emissions, possible collective phenomena). Selection by charged-particle multiplicity alone autocorrelates with jet activity, since high-multiplicity events are biased toward containing hard jets, and conversely jet fragmentation feeds the multiplicity.

Devise an experimental observable, event classifier, or analysis procedure (for instance based on event topology, transverse-region decompositions à la "toward/away/transverse," jet veto or jet tagging, transverse spherocity, R_T = N_ch^transverse / ⟨N_ch^transverse⟩, multiplicity in a forward region uncorrelated from a midrapidity probe region, or another rapidity/azimuth/p_T-based construction) that achieves the following:

(a) it separates, on an event-by-event basis or in a statistically controlled way, the contribution of hard processes (in particular jets and multi-parton interactions) from the contribution of soft processes to high-multiplicity pp final states at √s = 7 TeV;

(b) it produces a high-multiplicity event class whose properties (e.g. p_T spectra, identified-particle yields, long-range angular correlations, strangeness enhancement) can be tested for genuine soft-physics or collective-type behavior with quantifiably reduced bias from jet fragmentation;

(c) it admits an unambiguous comparison with Monte Carlo generators (e.g. PYTHIA, HERWIG) so that the residual jet contamination in the soft-selected sample, and the residual soft contamination in the hard-selected sample, can be quantified.

Specify the observable and its construction precisely, and quantify the achievable hard/soft discrimination power as a function of N_ch in pp collisions at √s = 7 TeV.

Let $(G, +)$ be a semigroup (a set equipped with an associative binary operation $+$). For an integer $n \ge 2$, an $n$-tuple of distribution functions $F_1, \ldots, F_n$ on $G$ is said to be jointly mixable if there exist $G$-valued random variables $X_1, \ldots, X_n$ with $X_i \sim F_i$ for each $i$ such that the sum $X_1 + X_2 + \cdots + X_n$ is almost surely constant (equal to a fixed element of $G$). A distribution $F$ on $G$ is said to be $n$-completely mixable if the $n$-tuple $(F, F, \ldots, F)$ is jointly mixable.

Investigate how the algebraic structure of the semigroup $G$ governs complete mixability and joint mixability on $G$. Specifically, characterize which properties of $G$ (e.g., commutativity, cancellativity, the existence of identities or inverses, group versus monoid versus general semigroup structure, finiteness, ordering, divisibility) determine:
(a) necessary and sufficient conditions on $F_1, \ldots, F_n$ for joint mixability;
(b) necessary and sufficient conditions on $F$ and $n$ for $n$-complete mixability;
(c) the set of admissible "center" constants $c \in G$ that can arise as the almost-sure value of $X_1 + \cdots + X_n$.

Develop a general theory linking the semigroup-theoretic properties of $G$ to the structure of completely and jointly mixable distributions, extending the known theory on $G = \mathbb{R}$ (with addition).

Propose a precise, formal criterion that distinguishes "interesting" or "natural" mathematical questions from arbitrary ones. Concretely, give a mathematically rigorous definition of what it means for a statement (formulated in some fixed formal language, such as the language of set theory or a sufficiently expressive arithmetic) to be an "interesting" or "natural" mathematical question, in such a way that:

(a) the definition can itself be stated within a formal framework (for example, using notions from algorithmic information theory such as Kolmogorov complexity, logical depth, or descriptive complexity relative to a fixed universal Turing machine);

(b) the definition agrees, on a substantial corpus of historical examples, with the informal mathematical judgment of which questions (e.g. the Riemann hypothesis, P vs NP, the continuum hypothesis, classification of finite simple groups) are considered natural or interesting and which (e.g. random encodings of arbitrary bit-strings as Diophantine statements) are not;

(c) the definition is invariant, up to a controlled additive or multiplicative constant, under reasonable changes of the underlying formal language or universal machine.

Either provide such a criterion and prove its properties, or prove that no criterion satisfying (a)–(c) can exist.

Let G be a finitely presented group with a finite generating set S, and let B_S(n) denote the number of elements of G expressible as a product of at most n elements of S ∪ S^{-1}. The growth function n ↦ B_S(n) is said to be:
- of polynomial growth if there exist constants C, d > 0 such that B_S(n) ≤ C n^d for all n ≥ 1;
- of exponential growth if there exists a constant a > 1 such that B_S(n) ≥ a^n for all sufficiently large n;
- of intermediate growth otherwise (i.e., faster than every polynomial but slower than every exponential function).

Up to the standard equivalence of growth functions, this classification does not depend on the choice of finite generating set S.

Question: Does every finitely presented group have either polynomial growth or exponential growth? Equivalently, does there exist a finitely presented group of intermediate growth?

Let $V =  igoplus_{n\in\mathbb{Z}} V_{(n)}$ be a simple vertex operator algebra over $\mathbb{C}$ with vertex operator map $Y:V\otimes V	o V[[x,x^{-1}]]$ and vacuum $\mathbf{1}$. Assume:

(a) $V_{(0)} = \mathbb{C}\mathbf{1}$ and $V_{(n)} = 0$ for $n<0$;

(b) the contragredient module $V'$ is isomorphic to $V$ as a $V$-module;

(c) $V$ is $C_2$-cofinite, i.e. $\dim V/C_2(V) < \infty$, where $C_2(V)$ is the subspace of $V$ spanned by the elements $\operatorname{Res}_x x^{-2} Y(u,x)v$ for $u,v\in V$;

(d) every grading-restricted generalized $V$-module is completely reducible.

Let $G$ be a finite group of automorphisms of $V$. For each $g\in G$, consider the category of $g$-twisted (grading-restricted generalized) $V$-modules, and consider the spaces of twisted intertwining operators of types $ inom{W_3}{W_1\,W_2}$ where $W_i$ is a $g_i$-twisted $V$-module.

Prove that these twisted intertwining operators satisfy the associativity, commutativity, and modular invariance properties (i.e. products and iterates of twisted intertwining operators converge absolutely on suitable domains and admit analytic continuations realizing associativity and commutativity isomorphisms, and the $q$-traces/pseudo-traces of products of twisted intertwining operators span a modular-invariant space under $SL(2,\mathbb{Z})$).

Setup. In the Quantum Theory of Atoms in Molecules (QTAIM), a molecular system described by an electronic wavefunction Ψ and total electron density ρ(r) is partitioned into disjoint topological atoms Ω_A, where each Ω_A is a basin of the gradient vector field of ρ bounded by zero-flux interatomic surfaces S(Ω_A) satisfying ∇ρ(r)·n(r) = 0 for all r on S(Ω_A). Within each atomic basin one defines the kinetic energy T(Ω_A), the potential energy V(Ω_A), and the atomic energy E(Ω_A). At an equilibrium nuclear geometry the Hellmann–Feynman forces F_A on every nucleus vanish, and one has the atomic virial theorem 2T(Ω_A) + V(Ω_A) = 0, which permits the identification E(Ω_A) = −T(Ω_A) and yields a consistent additive partition of the molecular energy E = Σ_A E(Ω_A).

Problem. Construct an extension of the QTAIM subsystem virial and energy analysis to arbitrary (non-equilibrium) nuclear configurations at which the Hellmann–Feynman forces F_A on the nuclei are non-zero. Specifically, define for each topological atom Ω_A a kinetic-energy functional T(Ω_A), a potential-energy functional V(Ω_A) (incorporating contributions arising from the non-vanishing nuclear forces), and an atomic energy functional E(Ω_A) that:

(i) reduce to the standard QTAIM definitions when all F_A = 0;
(ii) satisfy a generalized atomic virial relation that consistently accounts for the non-zero forces on the nuclei contained in or bounding Ω_A;
(iii) give an additive decomposition E = Σ_A E(Ω_A) of the total electronic (or total molecular) energy at the chosen non-equilibrium geometry;
(iv) are invariant under origin translation and behave consistently along nuclear displacements such as vibrational distortions and reaction-path geometries.

Either provide such a construction together with proofs of (i)–(iv), or show that no construction satisfying (i)–(iv) can exist.

Within the quantum theory of atoms in molecules, an "atom in a molecule" (a topological atom) is defined as a region Ω of three-dimensional position space bounded by a surface of zero flux of the gradient of the one-electron density ρ(r); that is, ∇ρ(r)·n(r) = 0 for every point r on ∂Ω, where n(r) is the outward unit normal. Atomic properties (e.g., electronic population, kinetic and potential energies, multipole moments, localization/delocalization indices) are computed as integrals over Ω of suitable density-derived operators or of reduced density matrices restricted to Ω.

The notion of the "state" of a physical system is taken to mean a specification from which all accessible properties of the system can, in principle and in practice, be deduced.

Problem. Provide the most efficient definition of the state of a topological atom Ω. The definition must:
(i) specify explicitly what mathematical object(s) (for instance, the restriction of the molecular one-electron density and/or higher-order reduced density matrices to Ω, an atomic wavefunction, a density matrix on a suitable Hilbert space associated with Ω, or another construct) constitute the atomic state;
(ii) be such that all standard atomic properties of Ω can be computed in practice from this object, by means of explicit, well-defined functionals;
(iii) avoid being a mere enumeration of all atomic properties, and avoid being a purely formal existence argument (such as appealing to the fact that the molecular one-electron density on Ω determines, in principle via a Hohenberg–Kohn-type theorem, all properties of the atom) without yielding a practical computational scheme.

Consider a four-parameter family of polynomials $\{Q_n(z;\mu,\nu,\alpha,\theta)\}_{n\ge 0}$ in the real variable $z$, taken to be orthogonal on (a subset of) the real line, together with a companion four-parameter family $\{G_n(z;\mu,\nu,\alpha,\beta)\}_{n\ge 0}$ which is a discrete-variable version of the same class. Both families are specified solely by a three-term recursion relation of the form
$$z\,P_n(z) = a_n(\text{params})\,P_{n+1}(z) + b_n(\text{params})\,P_n(z) + c_n(\text{params})\,P_{n-1}(z),$$
together with the initial conditions $P_{-1}\equiv 0$ and $P_0\equiv 1$, where the coefficients $a_n,b_n,c_n$ are explicit rational functions of $n$ and the four parameters that produce a positive-definite Jacobi operator on the appropriate parameter domain.

Determine, in closed analytic form, the following objects associated with each of the families $Q_n$ and $G_n$:

(a) the orthogonality measure (weight function) on the real line, including its support and normalization;
(b) a generating function $\sum_{n\ge 0} Q_n(z;\mu,\nu,\alpha,\theta)\,t^n$ (and analogously for $G_n$);
(c) the large-$n$ asymptotic behavior of $Q_n(z;\mu,\nu,\alpha,\theta)$ and $G_n(z;\mu,\nu,\alpha,\beta)$, both inside and outside the spectrum;
(d) the explicit orthogonality relation, including the squared-norm formula;
(e) a Rodrigues-type formula expressing the polynomials via repeated differentiation (or differencing) of the weight;
(f) forward and backward shift operator relations relating the polynomial of degree $n$ to that of degree $n\pm 1$ with shifted parameters;
(g) the location and asymptotic distribution of the zeros;
(h) a closed-form representation, ideally as a terminating generalized hypergeometric function ${}_pF_q$ (or a basic hypergeometric ${}_p\phi_q$ in the discrete case), expressing $Q_n$ and $G_n$ explicitly in $n$ and the parameters.

Consider relativistic heavy-ion collisions parameterized by center-of-mass energy per nucleon pair √s_NN, and define the strangeness enhancement factor E(√s_NN) as the ratio of the yield of strange hadrons (e.g. K, Λ, Ξ, Ω) per participating nucleon in nucleus–nucleus collisions to the corresponding yield in elementary nucleon–nucleon collisions at the same energy. Distinguish three energy regimes:

(i) the low-energy regime, in which the production of strange hadrons proceeds predominantly through sub-threshold mechanisms (multi-step hadronic processes and Fermi motion contributions below the free nucleon–nucleon production threshold);

(ii) the intermediate-energy regime, in which partonic (quark–gluon) degrees of freedom are expected to play an increasingly important role in the early stage of the collision;

(iii) the high-energy regime, in which a deconfined partonic medium is established.

Determine whether the function E(√s_NN), or its energy derivative, exhibits a distinct onset (a sharp change of behavior or a threshold-like feature) in the transition region between regimes (i) and (ii) that can be attributed to the emergence of partonic degrees of freedom; or whether such an onset is necessarily smeared out by additional hadronic medium effects (rescattering, hadronic re-interactions, canonical suppression, finite-volume effects) so that no sharp feature survives in observable strange-hadron yields. Provide either an experimentally identifiable signature establishing such an onset or a theoretical argument showing that it is washed out.

Thompson's group $F$ is the group of orientation-preserving piecewise linear homeomorphisms $f:[0,1]\to[0,1]$ such that:
\begin{itemize}
\item $f$ has only finitely many breakpoints, all of which lie in the dyadic rationals $\mathbb{Z}[1/2]\cap(0,1)$;
\item on each linear piece, the slope of $f$ is an integer power of $2$.
\end{itemize}
Equivalently, $F$ admits the finite presentation
\[ F = \langle x_0, x_1 \mid [x_0 x_1^{-1}, x_0^{-1} x_1 x_0],\; [x_0 x_1^{-1}, x_0^{-2} x_1 x_0^{2}] \rangle. \]

A discrete group $G$ is called \emph{amenable} if there exists a finitely additive probability measure $\mu:\mathcal{P}(G)\to[0,1]$ that is invariant under left translation, i.e.\ $\mu(gA) = \mu(A)$ for every $g \in G$ and every $A \subseteq G$. Equivalently (Følner's criterion), $G$ is amenable if for every finite subset $S \subseteq G$ and every $\varepsilon > 0$ there exists a non-empty finite subset $F \subseteq G$ such that $|sF \triangle F| < \varepsilon |F|$ for all $s \in S$.

Determine whether Thompson's group $F$, as defined above, is amenable.

Let $G$ be a finitely generated group, and let $\mathrm{Diff}^\infty_+(S^1)$ denote the group of orientation-preserving $C^\infty$ diffeomorphisms of the circle $S^1$, equipped with the $C^\infty$ topology. A homomorphism $\varphi : G \to \mathrm{Diff}^\infty_+(S^1)$ is called an action of $G$ on $S^1$ by $C^\infty$ diffeomorphisms; it is faithful if it is injective. A nonempty closed $\varphi(G)$-invariant subset $K \subset S^1$ is minimal if every $\varphi(G)$-orbit in $K$ is dense in $K$; such an action is said to be minimal when $K = S^1$, and to admit an exceptional minimal set when $K$ is a Cantor set (i.e. compact, perfect, totally disconnected, and properly contained in $S^1$).

Suppose $\varphi_0 : G \to \mathrm{Diff}^\infty_+(S^1)$ is a faithful action that admits an exceptional minimal set. Does there exist a family $(\varphi_t)_{t \in [0,1]}$ of faithful actions $\varphi_t : G \to \mathrm{Diff}^\infty_+(S^1)$ such that:

1. For every generator $g \in G$, the map $t \mapsto \varphi_t(g)$ is continuous from $[0,1]$ into $\mathrm{Diff}^\infty_+(S^1)$ with its $C^\infty$ topology;
2. $\varphi_t$ admits an exceptional minimal set for every $t \in [0,1)$;
3. $\varphi_1$ is a minimal action on $S^1$?

Let $\mathfrak{g}$ be a finite-dimensional Lie algebra over $\mathbb{C}$ (or $\mathbb{R}$) with Lie–Poisson bracket on $\mathfrak{g}^*$. For each $a\in\mathfrak{g}^*$ define the "frozen-argument" constant Poisson bracket $\{\cdot,\cdot\}_a$ on $\mathfrak{g}^*$ by $\{f,g\}_a(x):=\langle a,[df(x),dg(x)]\rangle$. Together with the Lie–Poisson bracket $\{\cdot,\cdot\}$ this gives a compatible Poisson pencil $\{\cdot,\cdot\}+\lambda\{\cdot,\cdot\}_a$. At a generic point $x\in\mathfrak{g}^*$ and for a generic covector $a\in\mathfrak{g}^*$, the corresponding pencil of skew-symmetric bilinear forms on $\mathfrak{g}$ admits a Jordan–Kronecker canonical decomposition into Kronecker blocks (with Kronecker indices) and Jordan blocks (with eigenvalues and sizes). The collection of sizes of Kronecker blocks together with the sizes of Jordan blocks (over each generic eigenvalue) is called the Jordan–Kronecker invariant of $\mathfrak{g}$.

Determine the Jordan–Kronecker invariants for the following classes of Lie algebras:
(a) semidirect sums $\mathfrak{g}\ltimes_\rho V$, where $\rho:\mathfrak{g}\to\mathrm{End}(V)$ is a representation of a semisimple Lie algebra $\mathfrak{g}$ and $V$ is regarded as a commutative ideal;
(b) Borel subalgebras of simple Lie algebras;
(c) parabolic subalgebras of simple Lie algebras;
(d) centralizers $\mathfrak{z}_\mathfrak{g}(a)$ of singular elements $a\in\mathfrak{g}$, where $\mathfrak{g}$ is simple;
(e) Lie algebras of small dimension.

Develop a theoretically grounded analytical framework for studying diversity in microbial communities that simultaneously accomplishes the following objectives:

1. Structure of diversity. Given genomic and ecological data from a microbial community (for example, populations such as E. coli or marine cyanobacteria like Prochlorococcus sampled from natural habitats), provide quantitative methods that infer the structure of genetic and phenotypic diversity within the community and identify the relative contributions of the principal evolutionary forces — mutation, recombination, selection (including frequency- and density-dependent selection), genetic drift, migration, and horizontal gene transfer — to that observed structure.

2. Organizational and temporal scales. Provide principled criteria for identifying the relevant organizational scales (e.g., single nucleotide, gene, operon, genome, strain, ecotype, species, community) and the relevant temporal scales (e.g., generational, ecological, evolutionary) at which diversity should be described and modeled, so that coarse-graining at these scales preserves the dynamics needed to explain the data.

3. Dynamical regime classification. Provide statistical tests or model-selection procedures, derived from explicit stochastic eco-evolutionary dynamical models, that decide between the following hypotheses for an observed pattern of diversity:
   (a) the diversity is stable, i.e., it corresponds to a stationary distribution of the underlying eco-evolutionary process;
   (b) the diversity is inherently transient, i.e., it reflects non-stationary relaxation toward (or away from) such a distribution;
   (c) the diversity is intrinsically generated by properties of the dynamical system itself (e.g., chaotic, neutral, or quasi-neutral attractors, clonal interference, eco-evolutionary feedback), independently of external forcing;
   (d) the diversity is adiabatically coupled to a changing environment, i.e., the community tracks slow environmental variation while remaining close to a moving quasi-equilibrium of the eco-evolutionary dynamics.

Specify the class of mathematical models (e.g., stochastic differential equations, individual-based models, coalescent or branching process models with ecological feedback) within which the framework is formulated, the inference procedures linking these models to high-throughput sequencing and ecological data, and the identifiability conditions under which the four regimes (a)–(d) can be distinguished from finite samples.

Let $F=\mathbb{F}_q$ be a finite field of characteristic $p$ and order $q=p^n$, and let $\psi\colon F\to\mathbb{C}^\times$ be the canonical additive character $\psi(x)=\exp(2\pi i\,\mathrm{Tr}_{F/\mathbb{F}_p}(x)/p)$. An integer $d$ is called an invertible exponent over $F$ if $\gcd(d,q-1)=1$, so that $x\mapsto x^d$ permutes $F$. For such $d$ and for $a\in F$, define the Weil sum of the binomial $x^d-ax$ by
$$W_{F,d}(a)\;=\;\sum_{x\in F}\psi(x^d-ax).$$
The Weil spectrum of $(F,d)$ is the multiset $\{W_{F,d}(a):a\in F^\ast\}$, and the pair $(F,d)$ is called three-valued if this multiset has exactly three distinct values.

Two invertible exponents $d,e$ over $F$ are declared equivalent if there exists an integer $k$ with either $d\equiv p^k e\pmod{q-1}$ or $d\equiv p^k e^{-1}\pmod{q-1}$ (where $e^{-1}$ denotes the inverse of $e$ modulo $q-1$); under this relation, $W_{F,d}$ and $W_{F,e}$ have the same spectrum.

The following infinite families of three-valued pairs $(F=\mathbb{F}_{p^n},d)$ are known (each up to the above equivalence):
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $p=2$, $d=2^k+1$ with $n/\gcd(n,k)$ odd (Gold);
\item[(ii)] $p=2$, $d=2^{2k}-2^k+1$ with $n/\gcd(n,k)$ odd (Kasami–Welch);
\item[(iii)] $p=3$, $n$ odd, $d=2\cdot 3^{(n-1)/2}+1$ (Welch-type);
\item[(iv)] $p=3$, $n$ odd, $d=3^r+2$ with $4r\equiv 1\pmod n$ (Dobbertin–Helleseth–Kumar–Martinsen / Niho-type for $p=3$);
\item[(v)] further sporadic and small-field families with the analogous three-valued property, as catalogued in the standard tabulation of three-valued Weil-sum pairs.
\end{enumerate}

Question: Is every pair $(F,d)$ with $W_{F,d}$ three-valued equivalent (under the above relation) to a member of one of these listed families? Equivalently, is the known list of three-valued Weil-sum families complete?

Let $X$ be a compact Kähler manifold and let $\mathcal{F}$ be a holomorphic foliation on $X$, possibly with a singular set $\mathrm{Sing}(\mathcal{F})$ of complex codimension at least $2$, whose leaves are immersed complex submanifolds of complex dimension $k$ with $k \geq 2$. Recall that in the case $k = 1$ (foliations by Riemann surfaces), a central analytic tool for ergodic theory is the notion of a directed $dd^c$-closed current: a positive current $T$ of bidimension $(1,1)$ on $X$ such that $T$ is directed by $\mathcal{F}$ (i.e.\ $T \wedge \omega = 0$ for any local holomorphic $1$-form $\omega$ defining $\mathcal{F}$ on a flow box) and $dd^c T = 0$. Such currents play the role of invariant measures and underlie the ergodic theory of singular holomorphic foliations by curves on compact Kähler manifolds.

Problem. Identify the appropriate replacement, in the higher-dimensional-leaf setting $k \geq 2$, for the notion of a directed $dd^c$-closed current. That is, define a class of positive currents (or other geometric/measure-theoretic objects) on $X$ associated to $\mathcal{F}$ which:
(i) reduces, in a natural sense, to directed $dd^c$-closed currents when $k = 1$;
(ii) is "directed" by the foliation $\mathcal{F}$ in a way compatible with its $k$-dimensional tangent distribution;
(iii) satisfies a closedness/harmonicity condition substituting $dd^c$-closedness;
(iv) supports the development of an ergodic theory for $\mathcal{F}$ analogous to the one available in the case of leaves of dimension $1$.

Give such a notion together with the basic structural properties (existence, compactness of the cone of such currents, support and extension across $\mathrm{Sing}(\mathcal{F})$, and a corresponding ergodic decomposition) that justify it as the correct higher-dimensional analogue.

Consider model atmospheres of early B-type and late O-type stars (effective temperatures roughly 20{,}000–35{,}000 K), in which the populations of ionic species are computed from the radiative-transfer equation coupled with statistical-equilibrium (non-LTE) rate equations. Two classes of models are commonly used:

(a) plane-parallel hydrostatic models, in which the atmosphere is treated as a one-dimensional layered slab in hydrostatic equilibrium, neglecting stellar wind and magnetic fields;

(b) extended spherical models, which incorporate spherical geometry, a hydrodynamic stellar wind (mass loss producing a velocity field v(r)), and, where present, a stellar magnetic field B affecting level populations and line transfer.

Two independent procedures are used to determine the effective temperature T_eff and surface gravity log g:

(i) the ionization-balance method, which fits T_eff and log g by requiring that abundances derived from successive ionization stages of C, N, and O (in particular, lines of C II/C III, N II/N III, O II/O III) agree;

(ii) the photometric / Balmer-line method, which fits T_eff and log g from broad-band photometric indices and the wings of hydrogen Balmer lines.

In observed early B and late O stars, method (i) systematically yields different values of (T_eff, log g) than method (ii), and the C II, N II, O II lines exhibit "superionization" — populations of these singly ionized species are lower than predicted by the plane-parallel non-LTE models, distorting the inferred abundances of carbon, nitrogen, and oxygen.

Problem. Construct a self-consistent atmospheric model for early B-type and late O-type stars that:

1. quantitatively reproduces the observed superionization of C II, N II, and O II;

2. eliminates the systematic discrepancy between (T_eff, log g) values determined by the ionization-balance method (i) and by the photometric / Balmer-line method (ii);

3. yields, simultaneously and consistently, reliable photospheric abundances of C, N, and O.

Specify the geometric assumptions, the wind hydrodynamics (mass-loss rate, velocity law), the treatment of the magnetic field, and the non-LTE radiative-transfer scheme required for such a model, and demonstrate that the resulting line spectrum and continuum are consistent with observations across this temperature range.

Let $R_d$ denote the space of rational maps of the Riemann sphere $\widehat{\mathbb{C}}$ of degree $d \ge 2$, equipped with the topology of uniform convergence (equivalently, convergence of coefficients up to a common normalization). Recall that an invariant Herman ring of a rational map $f \in R_d$ is an $f$-invariant Fatou component $A_f \subset \widehat{\mathbb{C}}$ that is conformally isomorphic to a round annulus $\{1<|z|<r\}$ in such a way that $f|_{A_f}$ is conjugate, via this isomorphism, to an irrational rotation $z\mapsto e^{2\pi i \alpha}z$; the number $\alpha\in\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}$ is called the rotation number of the ring.

Fix an irrational $\alpha\in\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}$ satisfying a Diophantine condition, i.e., there exist constants $C>0$ and $\tau\ge 0$ such that $|\alpha - p/q|\ge C\,q^{-2-\tau}$ for all integers $p,q$ with $q\ge 1$. Fix a base map $f_0\in R_d$ that has an invariant Herman ring $A_{f_0}$ with rotation number $\alpha$.

Define $H_{d,\alpha}\subset R_d$ to be the set of all $f_1\in R_d$ for which there exists a continuous path $t\mapsto f_t$, $t\in[0,1]$, in $R_d$ from $f_0$ to $f_1$ such that:
\begin{itemize}
\item for every $t\in[0,1]$, $f_t$ has an invariant Herman ring $A_{f_t}$ with rotation number $\alpha$;
\item the family of annuli $\{A_{f_t}\}_{t\in[0,1]}$ varies continuously with $t$ in the sense of Carathéodory convergence of pointed domains.
\end{itemize}

Answer the following two questions:

(a) Is $H_{d,\alpha}$ locally closed in $R_d$ (i.e., open in its closure $\overline{H_{d,\alpha}}\subset R_d$)?

(b) Is the topological boundary of $H_{d,\alpha}$ inside $\overline{H_{d,\alpha}}$ a topological manifold?

Consider the task of multiparty quantum key agreement (MQKA): N spatially separated parties, connected by a quantum network with prescribed topology, wish to jointly establish a common secret key by sharing and locally measuring an N-partite entangled quantum resource state, possibly assisted by classical communication, in the presence of (i) photon loss along each channel modeled by independent bosonic pure-loss channels of transmissivity η, (ii) dephasing noise on each subsystem of rate γ, and (iii) device imperfections such as detector inefficiency, dark counts, and finite source visibility.

Standard resource families to consider include:
- N-qubit GHZ states |GHZ_N⟩ = (|0⟩^{⊗N} + |1⟩^{⊗N})/√2;
- N-qubit W states |W_N⟩ = (1/√N) Σ_i |0…1_i…0⟩;
- N-qubit cluster (and more general graph) states |G⟩ associated to a graph G;
- continuous-variable multimode squeezed (Gaussian) states such as CV-GHZ states obtained from squeezed vacua and beam-splitter networks;
- photonic graph states generated from single-photon or quantum-emitter sources;
- time-bin multiplexed entangled resources;
- "hybrid" states obtained as superpositions, tensor products, or continuous interpolations |Ψ(λ)⟩ = α(λ)|GHZ_N⟩ + β(λ)|W_N⟩ + … of the above families, including discrete-/continuous-variable hybrid encodings.

For each candidate resource family R, define performance functionals under the noise model above:
1. asymptotic secret-key rate K(R; η, γ, ε) per network use for an MQKA protocol;
2. output fidelity F(R; η) of the distributed state with its ideal target;
3. imperfection threshold ε*(R) (the maximal device-imperfection level for which K > 0);
4. generation/distribution scalability S(R, N), measured e.g. by the resource overhead required to produce R with fidelity ≥ 1 − δ over N nodes;
5. hardware-compatibility index H(R), encoding compatibility with available photonic, atomic, or superconducting platforms.

Problem. Identify and rigorously classify the multipartite resource families R — in particular hybrid families interpolating between GHZ, W, cluster/graph, CV squeezed, photonic graph, and time-bin multiplexed states — that strictly dominate the pure GHZ and pure W deployments in the MQKA setting above, in the sense that there exist nontrivial regimes of (N, η, γ, ε, network topology) where K(R) > max{K(|GHZ_N⟩), K(|W_N⟩)} while preserving comparable or better values of F, ε*, S, and H. Provide:
(a) explicit constructions of such dominant hybrid resources as a function of network topology and noise parameters;
(b) a classification theorem partitioning the resource landscape by the tuple (K, F, ε*, S, H);
(c) scalable measurement-based MQKA protocols for photonic graph states and time-bin multiplexed resources, together with their achievable key rates and loss/dephasing thresholds;
(d) tight upper bounds showing the limits of any such hybrid advantage.

A finite semigroup S with zero 0 is called 0-simple if S^2 ≠ {0} and the only ideals of S are {0} and S. For a class X of finite semigroups, define the following Birkhoff-type closure operators:
- H(X): the class of all homomorphic images of members of X;
- S(X): the class of all subsemigroups of members of X;
- P(X): the class of all finite direct products of members of X.
Compositions such as HP, HPS, and SHPS are defined by applying these operators in the indicated order (right to left). A class Y of finite semigroups is called decidable if there exists an algorithm that, given a finite semigroup T (presented, say, by its multiplication table), decides whether T ∈ Y.

Let C_0 denote the class of all finite 0-simple semigroups. For each of the operators K ∈ {HP, HPS, SHPS}, determine whether the class K(C_0) is decidable.

Let $M$ be a closed orientable 3-manifold that is a mod 2 homology 3-sphere (i.e., $H_*(M;\mathbb{Z}/2)\cong H_*(S^3;\mathbb{Z}/2)$), and let $G$ be a finite non-solvable group acting on $M$ by orientation-preserving diffeomorphisms. Denote by $O(G)$ the maximal normal subgroup of $G$ of odd order. It is known that $G/O(G)$ contains a normal subgroup $N$, with quotient $(G/O(G))/N$ either abelian or a 2-fold extension of an abelian group, such that $N$ is isomorphic to one of the following groups:

(i) $\mathrm{PSL}(2,q)$;
(ii) $\mathrm{PSL}(2,q)\times \mathbb{Z}_2$;
(iii) $\mathrm{SL}(2,q)\times_{\mathbb{Z}_2} C$, where $C$ is a finite solvable group with a unique involution and the central product is taken over the common central involution;
(iv) the unique non-split central extension $\widehat{A}_7$ of the alternating group $A_7$ by $\mathbb{Z}_2$;
(v) $\mathrm{SL}(2,q)\times_{\mathbb{Z}_2}\mathrm{SL}(2,q')$, the central product over the common central involution,

where $q,q'$ are odd prime powers strictly greater than $4$.

Determine, for each of the groups in the list (i)–(v):
(a) which of them actually admit an orientation-preserving action on some mod 2 homology 3-sphere;
(b) which of them occur as the orientation-preserving isometry group of a closed hyperbolic 3-manifold that arises as the 2-fold cyclic branched covering of $S^3$ along some knot.

Let $a$ and $b$ be positive integers, and let $U_{a,b}$ denote the uniform matroid of rank $a$ on $b$ elements (so $a\le b$). Consider the minor-closed class $\mathcal{M}(U_{a,b})$ consisting of all matroids that do not contain $U_{a,b}$ as a minor. 

Recall that for a finite group $\Gamma$ and a positive integer $r$, the rank-$r$ Dowling matroid $Q_r(\Gamma)$ is the matroid on the set of "joints" together with $\Gamma$-labeled "edges" arising from the $\Gamma$-gain graph $K_r^\Gamma$. Recall also that $U_{a,b}$ is representable over a finite field $\mathbb{F}_q$ iff there exist $b$ vectors in $\mathbb{F}_q^a$ in general position (every $a$ of them linearly independent).

Problem. Find a qualitative structure theorem, analogous in spirit to the Graph Minors Structure Theorem of Robertson and Seymour, that describes the class $\mathcal{M}(U_{a,b})$. Specifically, prove that every matroid with no $U_{a,b}$-minor can be built in a tree-like (clique-sum / tree-decomposition) fashion from "basic" pieces drawn from the following classes:
(i) Dowling matroids $Q_r(\Gamma)$ over finite groups $\Gamma$;
(ii) duals of Dowling matroids;
(iii) matroids representable over finite fields $\mathbb{F}_q$ over which $U_{a,b}$ is not representable;
together with bounded-size perturbations, in such a way that the basic classes used in the description do not themselves contain arbitrarily large uniform matroids as minors.

Let M be a matroid. The branch-width of M is defined via branch-decompositions in the standard way. The dual matroid is denoted M*, and U_{k,2k} denotes the uniform matroid of rank k on 2k elements. For a graph G, the cycle matroid M(G) is the matroid on E(G) whose independent sets are the edge sets of forests. The framed Dowling matroid B^+(G) of G is the matroid on V(G) ∪ E(G) in which V(G) is a basis, each non-loop edge e = uv is freely placed on the line spanned by its endpoints u and v, and each loop at v is placed in parallel with v. The bicircular matroid is B(G) := B^+(G) \ V(G).

Prove or disprove: For every positive integer k there exists a positive integer n such that every matroid M with branch-width at least n satisfies that either M or M* contains, as a minor, at least one of the following three matroids:
(i) U_{k,2k};
(ii) the cycle matroid of the k × k grid graph;
(iii) the bicircular matroid of the k × k grid graph.

Let $k$ be a field and $S=k[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ the standard graded polynomial ring, where each $x_i$ has degree $1$. The Hilbert function of a homogeneous ideal $J\subseteq S$ is the function $H_{S/J}:\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}\to\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}$ given by $H_{S/J}(d)=\dim_k (S/J)_d$. A regular sequence in $S$ is a sequence $f_1,\dots,f_j$ of homogeneous elements of positive degree such that, for each $i$, $f_i$ is a non-zero-divisor on $S/(f_1,\dots,f_{i-1})$.

Suppose $I\subseteq S$ is a homogeneous ideal that contains a regular sequence of homogeneous elements of degrees $a_1,a_2,\dots,a_j$ (with $1\leq j\leq n$ and $a_i\geq 1$). Does there exist a monomial ideal $M\subseteq S$ such that:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $M$ contains the pure powers $x_1^{a_1}, x_2^{a_2},\dots, x_j^{a_j}$, and
\item $M$ has the same Hilbert function as $I$, i.e., $H_{S/M}(d)=H_{S/I}(d)$ for every $d\geq 0$?
\end{enumerate}

Consider the unconstrained optimization problem
   min { f(x) : x ∈ ℝⁿ },
where f : ℝⁿ → ℝ is continuously differentiable and bounded below, with gradient g(x) = ∇f(x). A nonlinear conjugate gradient (CG) algorithm generates iterates x_{k+1} = x_k + α_k d_k, where the search direction is d_0 = −g_0 and, for k ≥ 1,
   d_k = −g_k + β_k d_{k−1},
for some scalar β_k (e.g., Fletcher–Reeves, Polak–Ribière–Polyak, Hestenes–Stiefel, Dai–Yuan, Hager–Zhang, etc.). The step length α_k > 0 is typically obtained by a line-search procedure, examples being:
 (i) exact line search: α_k = argmin_{α>0} f(x_k + α d_k);
 (ii) standard Wolfe conditions: f(x_k+α_k d_k) ≤ f(x_k) + c_1 α_k g_k^T d_k and g(x_k+α_k d_k)^T d_k ≥ c_2 g_k^T d_k, with 0 < c_1 < c_2 < 1;
 (iii) approximate Wolfe conditions of Hager–Zhang;
 (iv) non-monotone line-search conditions (e.g., Grippo–Lampariello–Lucidi type), in which the Armijo-type acceptance test compares f(x_k+α_k d_k) to max_{0≤j≤m(k)} f(x_{k−j}) instead of f(x_k).

Address the following questions rigorously:

(a) Quantify the influence of the accuracy of the line-search procedure on the convergence speed, robustness, and overall numerical performance of nonlinear CG algorithms. In particular, identify which level and which type of line-search accuracy among (i)–(iv) is most effective for a representative class of smooth (possibly nonconvex) unconstrained problems.

(b) Determine whether the non-monotone line search of type (iv) is more effective, in terms of efficiency and reliability, than the (standard or approximate) Wolfe line search of types (ii)–(iii) when used inside nonlinear CG schemes.

(c) Design a competitive nonlinear CG algorithm for unconstrained minimization that completely avoids any line-search procedure (i.e., uses a closed-form or otherwise computationally cheap rule for α_k that does not require evaluating f or g at trial points along d_k), prove its global convergence under standard assumptions (e.g., Lipschitz continuous gradient, bounded level sets), and show that it is competitive with state-of-the-art line-search-based CG methods on standard benchmarks.
